OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Filtered-chromatic-splitting-at-generic-primes-September-25-2026", "title": "Filtered chromatic splitting at generic primes", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Filtered-chromatic-splitting-at-generic-primes-September-25-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "318", "family_title": "Chromatic splitting: filtrations and counterexamples", "abstract_html": "For every <i>n</i> ≥ 1 and prime \\(p\\gt n+1\\), we construct a \\(2^n\\)-stage ordered filtration of \\(L_{n-1}L_{K(n)}S_p^\\wedge\\) with the classical chromatic-splitting cofibers. The map from the first stage to the target is the canonical localization unit.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Filtered chromatic splitting at generic primes", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(n\\geq 1\\), let \\(p>n+1\\) be prime, and put \\( S=S_p^\\wedge,\\qquad D=L_{K(n)}S,\\qquad X_{n,p}=L_{n-1}D. \\) Here \\(L_r=L_{E(r)}\\), and \\(L_0\\) is rationalization.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(n\\geq 1\\), let \\(p&gt;n+1\\) be prime, and put\n\\[\nS=S_p^\\wedge,\\qquad D=L_{K(n)}S,\\qquad X_{n,p}=L_{n-1}D.\n\\]\nHere \\(L_r=L_{E(r)}\\), and \\(L_0\\) is rationalization. Work in the category of \\(E(n-1)\\)-local \\(S\\)-modules, with equivalences and localizations detected on underlying spectra. For a finite set \\(I\\) of positive integers, set\n\\[\nd(I)=\\sum_{i\\in I}(2i-1),\\qquad d(\\varnothing)=0.\n\\]</p><p>There exist an enumeration \\(I_1,\\ldots,I_{2^n}\\) of the subsets of \\(\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}\\), a filtration \\(F_\\bullet\\), and classes\n\\[\ny_i\\in\\pi_{1-2i}D,\\qquad 1\\leq i\\leq n,\n\\]\nwith the following properties.</p><p>(a) \\(I_1=\\varnothing\\), and \\(\\max I_j\\) is nondecreasing for \\(j\\geq2\\). There is a filtration\n\\[\n0=F_0\\longrightarrow F_1\\longrightarrow\\cdots\\longrightarrow\nF_{2^n}\\xrightarrow{\\simeq}X_{n,p}\n\\]\nwhose successive cofibers are\n\\[\n\\operatorname{cofib}(F_{j-1}\\to F_j)\\simeq C_{I_j},\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nC_\\varnothing=L_{n-1}S,\\qquad\nC_I=\\Sigma^{-d(I)}L_{n-\\max I}S\\quad(I\\neq\\varnothing).\n\\]\nUnder \\(F_1=C_\\varnothing\\), the composite \\(F_1\\to X_{n,p}\\) is the canonical localization unit.</p><p>(b) Set \\(y_\\varnothing=1\\), and, for \\(I=\\{i_1&lt;\\cdots&lt;i_r\\}\\), set \\(y_I=y_{i_1}\\cdots y_{i_r}\\). For this one family and every \\(1\\leq t&lt;n\\), the specified map\n\\[\nL_{K(t)}\n\\left(\n\\bigvee_{I\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,n-t\\}}\n\\Sigma^{-d(I)}S\\xrightarrow{(y_I)}D\n\\right)\n\\]\nis an equivalence.</p><p>The last rational filtration block is chosen from rational dimensions; its individual pieces are not identified with products containing \\(y_n\\). The theorem does not assert a wedge decomposition or a retraction of the unit.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 14 supplies the one family \\(y_i\\), its ordered products, and \\(y_\\varnothing=1\\).</p><p>If \\(n&gt;1\\), put \\(z_I=y_I\\) for \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,n-1\\}\\), and let \\(u=y_\\varnothing:S\\to D\\) be the unit. Proposition 15 verifies every simultaneous positive-height basis hypothesis of Proposition 1. Proposition 16 verifies its rational dimension hypothesis. Proposition 1 constructs the quotient blocks, refines them into the \\(2^n\\) prescribed individual cofibers, and pulls them back to a filtration of \\(L_{n-1}D\\). Its first-stage map is exactly the localized unit. All maps and cofibers are \\(S\\)-linear.</p><p>If \\(n=1\\), the positive-height tests are vacuous. Proposition 14 still supplies \\(y_1\\). The nonzero rationalized height-one unit established in Proposition 1 and the dimensions of Proposition 16 verify the remaining criterion hypotheses. The two cofibers are \\(H\\mathbb Q_p\\) and \\(\\Sigma^{-1}H\\mathbb Q_p\\), with canonical first-stage unit.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Filtration criterion", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(n\\geq1\\), let \\(p\\) be odd, put \\(S=S_p^\\wedge\\), and let \\(D\\) be an \\(S\\)-module with a specified map \\(u:S\\to D\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(n\\geq1\\), let \\(p\\) be odd, put \\(S=S_p^\\wedge\\), and let \\(D\\) be an \\(S\\)-module with a specified map \\(u:S\\to D\\). For finite \\(I\\), put \\(d(I)=\\sum_{i\\in I}(2i-1)\\). Suppose maps\n\\[\nz_I:\\Sigma^{-d(I)}S\\longrightarrow D,\n\\qquad I\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,n-1\\},\\qquad z_\\varnothing=u,\n\\]\nsatisfy:</p><p>(a) For every \\(1\\leq t&lt;n\\),\n\\[\n\\bigvee_{I\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,n-t\\}}\n\\Sigma^{-d(I)}S\\xrightarrow{(z_I)}D\n\\]\nis a \\(K(t)\\)-equivalence.</p><p>(b) For every integer \\(b\\),\n\\[\n\\dim_{\\mathbb Q_p}\\pi_{-b}L_0D\n=\\#\\{I\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}:d(I)=b\\}.\n\\]</p><p>If \\(n=1\\), additionally assume \\(\\pi_0L_0u\\neq0\\).</p><p>Then \\(X=L_{n-1}D\\) has the filtration and unit compatibility stated in Theorem 1(a), with \\(u\\) in place of the unit.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the following localization facts:\n\\[\nL_{K(t)}S\\simeq L_{K(t)}L_rS\\quad(r\\geq t),\n\\]\nan \\(E(t)\\)-local object with zero \\(K(t)\\)-localization is \\(E(t-1)\\)-local, and\n\\[\nL_0L_rS=L_0S=H\\mathbb Q_p.\n\\]\nThe last equality follows from the finiteness of positive stable stems and the Moore and Milnor sequences for the \\(p\\)-complete sphere.</p><p>The rationalized unit of \\(L_{K(1)}S\\) is nonzero. Indeed, at odd \\(p\\), the homotopy-fixed-point spectral sequence for\n\\[\nE_1=KU_p^\\wedge,\\qquad\n\\mathbb Z_p^\\times=\\mu_{p-1}\\times(1+p\\mathbb Z_p)\n\\]\nhas cohomological dimension one. In total degree zero its only term is\n\\[\nH^0_{\\mathrm{cts}}(\\mathbb Z_p^\\times;\\pi_0E_1)=\\mathbb Z_p.\n\\]\nThe sphere unit maps to \\(1\\), so \\(\\pi_0L_{K(1)}S=\\mathbb Z_p\\), with nonzero rationalized unit.</p><p>Suppose \\(n&gt;1\\). Set \\(Q_0=X\\). The first block is\n\\[\nB_1=L_{n-1}S\\vee\\Sigma^{-1}L_{n-1}S.\n\\]\nLocalize \\(z_\\varnothing,z_{\\{1\\}}\\) to obtain \\(B_1\\to Q_0\\), and let \\(Q_1\\) be its cofiber. Hypothesis (a) at \\(t=n-1\\) makes \\(Q_1\\) \\(E(n-2)\\)-local.</p><p>Inductively, if \\(Q_{j-1}\\) is \\(E(n-j)\\)-local, the composites of the original \\(z_I\\), for \\(\\max I=j\\), extend by localization adjunction to give\n\\[\nB_j=\\bigvee_{\\max I=j}\\Sigma^{-d(I)}L_{n-j}S\n\\longrightarrow Q_{j-1}\\longrightarrow Q_j.\n\\]\nAt \\(t=n-j\\), the original specified basis is indexed by subsets of \\(\\{1,\\ldots,j\\}\\). Earlier blocks remove exactly the empty subset and subsets with maximum less than \\(j\\): each map into a quotient remains the composite of the same original component with that quotient map. Thus \\(L_{K(t)}(B_j\\to Q_{j-1})\\) is an equivalence, and \\(Q_j\\) is \\(E(t-1)\\)-local. This reaches a rational \\(Q_{n-1}\\).</p><p>The \\(z_I\\), \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,n-1\\}\\), are rationally independent in each degree. Under the specified \\(K(1)\\)-equivalence, they map to nonzero rationalized units in distinct shifted summands. This also separates subsets with equal \\(d(I)\\). Rationalizing each block therefore gives an injection into the quotient by earlier vectors, with no suspended kernel in the cofiber sequence. Hypothesis (b) consequently gives\n\\[\n\\dim_{\\mathbb Q_p}\\pi_{-b}Q_{n-1}\n=\\#\\{I:\\max I=n,\\ d(I)=b\\}.\n\\]\nChoose rational bases to obtain\n\\[\nB_n=\\bigvee_{\\max I=n}\\Sigma^{-d(I)}H\\mathbb Q_p\n\\xrightarrow{\\simeq}Q_{n-1},\n\\]\nand set \\(Q_n=0\\).</p><p>Set \\(G_j=\\operatorname{fib}(X\\to Q_j)\\). The cartesian square\n\\[\n\\begin{matrix}\nG_j&amp;\\longrightarrow&amp;B_j\\\\\n\\downarrow&amp;&amp;\\downarrow\\\\\nX&amp;\\longrightarrow&amp;Q_{j-1}\n\\end{matrix}\n\\]\ngives a cofiber sequence \\(G_{j-1}\\to G_j\\to B_j\\). Refine each finite wedge \\(B_j=C_1\\vee\\cdots\\vee C_r\\) by pullback along its partial wedges. Exactness of pullback gives individual cofibers \\(C_1,\\ldots,C_r\\). Choose the unit summand first in \\(B_1\\); its pullback is \\(L_{n-1}S\\), with map exactly the localized \\(u\\). The number of pieces is\n\\[\n2+\\sum_{j=2}^{n-1}2^{j-1}+2^{n-1}=2^n.\n\\]</p><p>If \\(n=1\\), \\(X=L_0D\\) has one \\(\\mathbb Q_p\\) in degrees \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\). The assumed nonzero \\(L_0u\\) is an isomorphism in degree zero, with cofiber \\(\\Sigma^{-1}H\\mathbb Q_p\\).</p><p>[Original Remark 2.2.] The pullbacks retain the boundary maps of the quotient construction; no nullhomotopies of attaching maps are required.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "[Finite labeled rings, full sections, and boundary identifications; original Proposition 3.1, Lemma 3.2, and Corollary 3.3.] Fix \\(1\\leq t<n<p-1\\), put \\(m=n-t\\), and choose a finite field \\(k\\) containing the Honda …", "statement": "<p>[Finite labeled rings, full sections, and boundary identifications; original Proposition 3.1, Lemma 3.2, and Corollary 3.3.]</p><p>Fix \\(1\\leq t&lt;n&lt;p-1\\), put \\(m=n-t\\), and choose a finite field \\(k\\) containing the Honda endomorphism fields at heights at most \\(n\\), with degree prime to \\(p\\). Put\n\\[\nA=k[[x_t,\\ldots,x_{n-1}]],\\qquad x=x_t,\\qquad B=A[1/x].\n\\]\nLet \\(\\mathcal G_A\\) be the characteristic-\\(p\\), starting-height-\\(t\\) quotient of the universal height-\\(n\\) formal deformation. Choose its coordinate so that, with \\(x_n=1\\),\n\\[\n[p](T)\\equiv x_iT^{p^i}\n\\pmod{(x_t,\\ldots,x_{i-1},T^{p^i+1})},\n\\]\nand \\([p](T)\\equiv T^{p^n}\\) modulo all parameters. Form the algebraic \\(p\\)-divisible group from its finite prepared kernels before localization.</p><p>For \\(l\\geq1\\), let \\(\\widetilde C_l\\) be the reduced generic closure of lifted bases of the étale \\(p^l\\)-torsion, with coordinates \\(z_{1,l},\\ldots,z_{m,l}\\), and set\n\\[\nw_{i,l}=z_{i,l}^{p^{tl}},\\qquad C_l=A[w_{1,l},\\ldots,w_{m,l}].\n\\]\nThen:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. \\(C_l=k[[w_{1,l},\\ldots,w_{m,l}]]\\) and\n\\(\\widetilde C_l=k[[z_{1,l},\\ldots,z_{m,l}]]\\), both finite free over \\(A\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. \\(C_l[1/x]\\) is the finite étale basis algebra, and the full lift algebra is\n\\[\n\\widetilde C_l[1/x]=(C_l[1/x])^{1/p^{tl}}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">3. \\(C_1\\) represents full labeled sections of the Frobenius-divided \\([p]\\)-kernel over arbitrary, including nilpotent, test rings.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. Vanishing independent labels give the higher-starting-height labeled discs after the stated finite root transports.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 2.1 establishes the finite rings and full lift schemes. Lemma 2.2 supplies their nilpotent-compatible full-section presentation. Lemma 2.3 supplies the boundary identifications and conormal actions used in the later induction.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Proposition 3.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In the setting of Proposition 2, \\( C_l=k[[w_{1,l},\\ldots,w_{m,l}]],\\qquad \\widetilde C_l=k[[z_{1,l},\\ldots,z_{m,l}]],\\qquad w_{i,l}=z_{i,l}^{p^{tl}}. \\) Both rings are finite free over \\(A\\).", "statement": "<p>In the setting of Proposition 2,\n\\[\nC_l=k[[w_{1,l},\\ldots,w_{m,l}]],\\qquad\n\\widetilde C_l=k[[z_{1,l},\\ldots,z_{m,l}]],\\qquad\nw_{i,l}=z_{i,l}^{p^{tl}}.\n\\]\nBoth rings are finite free over \\(A\\). Over \\(C_l[1/x]\\), the full scheme of lifts of the universal étale basis has algebra \\(\\widetilde C_l[1/x]\\), finite locally free of rank \\(p^{tlm}\\). Transition maps are finite and injective, and, for \\(j\\leq l\\),\n\\[\nz_{i,j}\\in\nk[[z_{1,l}^{p^{t(l-j)}},\\ldots,z_{m,l}^{p^{t(l-j)}}]].\n\\]\nFor the boundary convention \\(t=n\\), the empty labeled ring is \\(k\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The reduced generic closure is finite, has dimension \\(m\\), and every irreducible component dominates \\(\\operatorname{Spec}A\\). Its unique closed point has residue field \\(k\\).</p><p>Put \\(a_i=[p^{l-1}](z_{i,l})\\), and let \\(a_v\\) be the formal-group combination with label \\(v\\in\\mathbb F_p^m\\). For the distinguished polynomial \\(W_p\\),\n\\[\nW_p(T)=\\prod_{v\\in\\mathbb F_p^m}(T-a_v)^{p^t}\n=\\prod_{v\\in\\mathbb F_p^m}(T^{p^t}-a_v^{p^t}).\n\\]\nThis holds over geometric generic fields and hence over the reduced closure. Since \\([p^{l-1}]\\) factors through \\(T^{p^{t(l-1)}}\\), every \\(a_v^{p^t}\\) is a power series in the \\(w_{i,l}\\) with zero constant term. Modulo all \\(w_{i,l}\\), the displayed polynomial is \\(T^{p^n}\\). The height congruences successively give \\(x_t=\\cdots=x_{n-1}=0\\). Thus the maximal ideal of \\(C_l\\) is generated by its \\(m\\) displayed coordinates. Dimension and embedding dimension agree, so the power-series presentation is an isomorphism. The same argument gives the presentation of \\(\\widetilde C_l\\). Cohen–Macaulayness and the parameter sequence from \\(A\\) give finite freeness.</p><p>Write\n\\[\n[p^l](T)=g_l(T^{p^{tl}}),\\qquad g_l(S)=U_l(S)V_l(S),\n\\]\nwhere \\(V_l\\) is distinguished of degree \\(p^{ml}\\). The linear coefficient of \\(g_l\\) is a unit times \\(x^{1+p^t+\\cdots+p^{t(l-1)}}\\). The invariant-differential identity makes \\(V_l'\\) invertible after \\(x\\) is inverted. The divided group is therefore étale of rank \\(p^{ml}\\). Its basis algebra surjects onto \\(C_l[1/x]\\), is generically an isomorphism, and has no torsion kernel.</p><p>The full lift scheme is finite locally free of rank \\(p^{tlm}\\), and surjects onto \\(\\widetilde C_l[1/x]\\). The latter has the free basis\n\\[\n\\prod_i z_{i,l}^{e_i},\\qquad 0\\leq e_i&lt;p^{tl},\n\\]\nover \\(C_l[1/x]\\). Equality of ranks proves equality with the full, unreduced lift scheme.</p><p>Generic basis lifting proves injectivity of transitions. Finiteness follows from finiteness over \\(A\\), and the formula \\(z_{i,j}=[p^{l-j}](z_{i,l})\\) gives the stated Frobenius divisibility.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Integral full-section presentation", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\( H=\\mathcal G_A[p]/\\ker(F^t). \\) Then \\(C_1\\) represents full labeled group-homomorphism tuples \\(\\mathbb F_p^m\\to H\\), including over nilpotent test rings.", "statement": "<p>Let\n\\[\nH=\\mathcal G_A[p]/\\ker(F^t).\n\\]\nThen \\(C_1\\) represents full labeled group-homomorphism tuples \\(\\mathbb F_p^m\\to H\\), including over nilpotent test rings. Fullness means equality of the finite Cartier divisors, equivalently, after every base change, equality of the norm of every function with its product over the labeled sections.</p>", "proof": "<p>The divided prepared kernel is monogenic. Its full-section condition is a finite set of coefficient identities for its monic polynomial. Equality for the coordinate gives equality for every polynomial in that coordinate by substitution; every function has such a representative.</p><p>Let \\(Q\\) be the resulting finite complete \\(A\\)-algebra. It has one closed point. Setting its \\(m\\) labeled coordinates to zero makes the divided polynomial \\(T^{p^m}\\), and the original height congruences kill all height parameters. Its embedding dimension is therefore at most \\(m\\). The generic full tuple gives \\(Q\\twoheadrightarrow C_1\\). Since \\(C_1\\) has dimension \\(m\\), \\(Q\\) is regular of dimension \\(m\\). A same-dimensional quotient of this regular local domain has zero kernel.</p><p>All equations are formed in finite algebras before localization. Their later use requires polynomial operations, not evaluation of an unevaluated formal series at an order-one element.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.3", "orig": "Corollary 3.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every nonzero \\(v\\in\\mathbb F_p^m\\), there is a label coordinate \\(w_v\\) with \\( x=\\text{a unit}\\cdot\\prod_{0\\neq v\\in\\mathbb F_p^m}w_v. \\) Each \\((w_v)\\) is \\(P_n\\)-stable, where \\(P_n=\\mathcal O_{D_n}^{\\times}\\) …", "statement": "<p>For every nonzero \\(v\\in\\mathbb F_p^m\\), there is a label coordinate \\(w_v\\) with\n\\[\nx=\\text{a unit}\\cdot\\prod_{0\\neq v\\in\\mathbb F_p^m}w_v.\n\\]\nEach \\((w_v)\\) is \\(P_n\\)-stable, where \\(P_n=\\mathcal O_{D_n}^{\\times}\\), and its conormal is a power of the invariant coordinate line. For a primitive label, \\(C_1/(w_v)\\) is the starting-height-\\((t+1)\\), level-one ring with \\(p\\)-th roots of its parameters adjoined. This iterates for independent dead labels.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(a_v\\) be the labeled combination of the \\(z_{i,1}\\), and put \\(w_v=a_v^{p^t}\\). The coefficient of \\(T^{p^t}\\) in the factorization of Lemma 2.1 gives the product formula. Stabilizer transport is a coordinate change with invertible linear term, so \\(w_v\\) changes by a unit multiple; on its conormal only the linear coefficient to the \\(p^t\\)-th power remains.</p><p>Change labels so that \\(v\\) is first, and put \\(R=C_1/(w_1)=k[[w_2,\\ldots,w_m]]\\). The divided polynomial satisfies\n\\[\nQ_t(Y)=\\prod_{\\bar v\\in\\mathbb F_p^{m-1}}(Y^p-w_{\\bar v}^{p}).\n\\]\nSince \\(x_t=0\\), uniqueness of preparation gives \\(Q_t(Y)=Q_{t+1}(Y^p)\\), hence\n\\[\nQ_{t+1}(Z)=\\prod_{\\bar v}(Z-w_{\\bar v}^{p}).\n\\]\nLemma 2.2 identifies the higher-height ring with the subring\n\\(k[[w_2^p,\\ldots,w_m^p]]=R^p\\). Repeating gives the \\(p^h\\)-root transport for \\(h\\) independent dead labels.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "[Finite frame and lift torsors, powering, and Cartier transfers; original Proposition 3.4 and Lemmas 3.6–3.8.] Retain the setting of Proposition 2.", "statement": "<p>[Finite frame and lift torsors, powering, and Cartier transfers; original Proposition 3.4 and Lemmas 3.6–3.8.]</p><p>Retain the setting of Proposition 2. Let \\(\\Gamma_t\\) be the Honda height-\\(t\\) group. Joint frames consist of a connected marking\n\\[\n\\alpha:\\Gamma_t\\longrightarrow\\mathcal G_B^0\n\\]\nand an integral étale basis \\(i:\\mathbb Z_p^m\\to T_p\\mathcal G_B^{\\mathrm{et}}\\). Their group is\n\\[\nU_0=P_t\\times GL_m(\\mathbb Z_p),\\qquad\n(h,a)(\\alpha,i)=(\\alpha h^{-1},ia^{-1}).\n\\]\nFor open \\(U\\subseteq U_0\\), write \\(B_U\\) for the finite frame algebra and\n\\(\\mathcal B=\\operatorname*{colim}_U B_U\\).</p><p>The lift tower is a torsor for \\(\\mathcal T=(T_p\\Gamma_t)^m\\), with finite lift algebras \\(\\mathcal B^{1/p^{tr}}\\). Its normalized transfers are stationary Cartier operators after power transport, and their residue transposes are actual positive Frobenius on finite-cover functions, retaining the invariant-volume line.</p><p>[Original Definition 3.5.] Continuous cochains on compact profinite sources have one common pole bound placing their image in a parameter-adically continuous finite power-series lattice. For frame or root unions, one common finite stage is required. The cochain complex of a union is the filtered colimit of these finite-stage bounded-pole complexes. The same convention applies with an additional compact profinite parameter.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 3.1 constructs the torsors. Lemma 3.2 supplies their distribution models and normalizations. Lemma 3.3 identifies powered diagrams naturally. Lemma 3.4 identifies the normalized transfers and their actual Frobenius transposes.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "Proposition 3.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The \\(B_U\\) are finite étale over \\(B\\); normal-level transitions are finite étale deck-group torsors.", "statement": "<p>The \\(B_U\\) are finite étale over \\(B\\); normal-level transitions are finite étale deck-group torsors. Their \\(P_n\\)-action commutes with \\(U_0\\). Over \\(\\mathcal B\\), compatible basis lifts form an affine faithfully flat torsor for\n\\[\n\\mathcal T=(T_p\\Gamma_t)^m,\\qquad\nT_p\\Gamma_t=\\varprojlim_{[p]}\\Gamma_t[p^r].\n\\]\nIts quotient by \\([p]^r\\mathcal T\\) has group \\(\\Gamma_t[p^r]^m\\) and algebra\n\\[\n\\mathcal B^{1/p^{tr}}.\n\\]\nThe full lift algebra is \\(\\mathcal B^{\\mathrm{perf}}\\). The group \\(P_n\\) fixes the translation parameters, and\n\\[\n(h,a):\\tau\\longmapsto h\\tau a^{-1}.\n\\]\nEvery finite torsor diagram descends to a finite frame level containing its marking data.</p>", "proof": "<p>Exact-height formal-group classification makes the full connected-isomorphism ring a union of finite étale extensions. Its finite stages record coefficients of full markings satisfying their extension equations, not arbitrary bud isomorphisms. The first coefficient satisfies a tame equation \\(c^{p^t-1}=x^{-1}\\), up to the chosen leading unit; subsequent free coefficients satisfy additive equations with invertible linear coefficient.</p><p>Combine this marking tower with the étale-basis tower of Proposition 2. Universal deformation transport commutes with frame change.</p><p>At finite level, two lifts differ by an element of \\(\\Gamma_t[p^r]^m\\). Proposition 2 identifies the lift algebra with the \\(p^{tr}\\)-root algebra; relative Frobenius commutes with finite étale base change. The identification is canonical because a root is uniquely determined by its power. Affine inverse limits preserve the torsor identity and faithful flatness. Shifting compatible sequences identifies the kernel of projection with \\([p]^r\\mathcal T\\).</p><p>Transport by \\(P_n\\) carries marking, basis, and lift together, fixing their difference in the Honda group. Frame change gives the displayed translation action. Finite kernels and morphisms use finitely many marking coefficients.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "Divisible Frobenius levels", "depth": 1, "snippet": "If \\(t>1\\), put \\(d=m(t-1)\\).", "statement": "<p>If \\(t&gt;1\\), put \\(d=m(t-1)\\). Rational \\(\\mathcal T\\)-representations correspond to discrete modules locally killed by powers of the augmentation ideal of\n\\[\n\\Lambda=\\varprojlim_r\\mathcal O(\\Gamma_t[p^r]^m)^\\vee\n=\\mathcal O((\\widehat{\\Gamma_t^\\vee})^m)\n\\simeq k[[D_1,\\ldots,D_d]].\n\\]\nThere is \\(r_0&gt;0\\), divisible by \\(t-1\\), such that for \\(r=ar_0\\),\n\\[\nQ=p^{tr},\\qquad q=p^{tr/(t-1)}\n\\]\nfix \\(k\\), and\n\\[\n\\mathcal O((\\Gamma_t^\\vee[p^r])^m)\n=\\Lambda_q=\\Lambda/(D_1^q,\\ldots,D_d^q),\\qquad q^d=Q^m.\n\\]\nThe embedding \\([p]^r:\\mathcal T\\hookrightarrow\\mathcal T\\) induces \\(D_j\\mapsto D_j^q\\) on distributions. The regular function representation\n\\(N_q=\\mathcal O(\\mathcal T/[p]^r\\mathcal T)\\) admits a normalized auxiliary-equivariant identification \\(N_q\\simeq\\Lambda_q\\), under which integration is augmentation.</p><p>If \\(t=1\\), \\(\\Gamma_1=\\widehat{\\mathbb G}_m\\), rational translation representations split into characters of \\((\\mathbb Q_p/\\mathbb Z_p)^m\\), invariants are exact, and transfer is the constant-character projection fixing constants. Put \\(d=0\\) in this case.</p>", "proof": "<p>The dual Honda group has dimension \\(t-1\\), height \\(t\\), and is connected for \\(t&gt;1\\). Honda’s relation and Cartier duality give\n\\[\nF^t=[p]^{t-1}\\quad\\text{on }\\Gamma_t^\\vee.\n\\]\nThus divisible \\([p]^r\\) is the stated Frobenius. Choose the progression also to fix \\(k\\).</p><p>Finite Hopf duality uses the perfect pairing \\((f,g)\\mapsto\\lambda(fg)\\), where \\(\\lambda\\) is invariant integration. In coordinates this is complementary-monomial pairing with normalized invariant-volume density. If \\(v\\) is the highest original-coordinate monomial, then \\(a\\mapsto a\\cdot v\\) identifies \\(\\Lambda_q\\) with \\(N_q\\): a nonzero kernel ideal would meet the socle, whose integral acts nontrivially on \\(v\\). Dimensions agree.</p><p>An auxiliary coordinate change acts on the socle by its tangent determinant to the \\((Q-1)\\)-st power, which is trivial because \\(Q\\) fixes \\(k\\). With \\(\\lambda(v)=1\\),\n\\[\n\\lambda(a\\cdot v)=\\varepsilon(a).\n\\]\nContinuity on this finite-dimensional space over finite \\(k\\) makes the auxiliary action factor through a finite group. The multiplicative-height assertion is its character decomposition.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.3", "orig": "Powering on associated diagrams", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Put \\(\\mathcal E=\\mathcal B^{\\mathrm{perf}}\\) and \\( \\mathcal V(M)=(\\mathcal E\\otimes_kM)^{\\mathcal T} \\) for a finite translation representation \\(M\\).", "statement": "<p>Put \\(\\mathcal E=\\mathcal B^{\\mathrm{perf}}\\) and\n\\[\n\\mathcal V(M)=(\\mathcal E\\otimes_kM)^{\\mathcal T}\n\\]\nfor a finite translation representation \\(M\\). At an allowed level \\(r\\), put \\(H=[p]^r\\mathcal T\\). Transport \\(M\\) to \\(H\\) by \\([p]^r:\\mathcal T\\simeq H\\), then coinduce to \\(\\mathcal T\\); denote this exact functor by \\(C_r\\).</p><p>The associated diagram for \\(C_rM\\) is the \\(Q\\)-root diagram for \\(M\\). Powering gives natural, \\(P_n\\)- and auxiliary-equivariant identifications\n\\[\n\\Phi_Q(f)=f^Q,\\qquad \\Phi_Q(cf)=c^Q\\Phi_Q(f).\n\\]\nThey apply to finite diagrams and after invariants in compatible open frame subgroups, preserving the cochain convention.</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(\\rho\\) is the translation coaction and \\(\\rho_H\\) its transported restriction, then\n\\[\n\\rho\\Phi_Q=(\\Phi_Q\\otimes1)\\rho_H.\n\\]\nPerfection makes \\(\\Phi_Q\\) bijective; it identifies\n\\(\\mathcal E^H=\\mathcal B^{1/Q}\\) with \\(\\mathcal E^{\\mathcal T}=\\mathcal B\\).</p><p>For \\(t&gt;1\\), \\(\\Lambda\\) is finite free over the image of \\(D_j\\mapsto D_j^q\\), so induction and coinduction are exact. At \\(t=1\\), use finite character decomposition. Descent and adjunction give\n\\[\n\\mathcal V(C_rM)\\simeq(\\mathcal E\\otimes_kM)^H.\n\\]\nThe coaction identity gives the comparison. Marking automorphisms commute with \\([p]^r\\), and ring automorphisms commute with powering. All formulas involve finite marking data and finitely many denominators.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.4", "orig": "Cartier transfer and transpose", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Choose sufficiently divisible levels and compatible normalized invariant coordinate volumes on the \\(m\\) Honda factors.", "statement": "<p>Choose sufficiently divisible levels and compatible normalized invariant coordinate volumes on the \\(m\\) Honda factors. Power transport gives a stationary top form \\(\\eta\\), defined at a finite frame level, \\(P_n\\)-invariant, with auxiliary line\n\\[\n\\langle\\eta\\rangle=\\det\\operatorname{Lie}(\\Gamma_t^m)^*.\n\\]\nIts \\(GL_m(\\mathbb Z_p)\\)-weight is \\(+1\\) in every diagonal row.</p><p>Normalized lift-torsor transfers, after stationary power identification, are iterates of\n\\[\nS(f)=\\frac{\\mathcal C_Q(f\\eta)}{\\eta},\\qquad\nS(c^Qf)=cS(f),\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\mathcal C_Q\\) is Cartier trace on continuous top forms. They are finite Hopf integration, not field trace.</p><p>Their residue transpose is positive Frobenius on finite-cover functions and trace duals, with the one volume line retained; before stationary identification it is inclusion into the next root ring. These statements commute with finite étale frame change, finite diagrams, and all stated actions.</p>", "proof": "<p>On a root chart write\n\\[\nR_0=k[[w_1,\\ldots,w_m]],\\quad\nR_1=k[[z_1,\\ldots,z_m]],\\quad w_i=z_i^Q,\n\\]\nand \\(\\eta_1=a(z)\\,dz_1\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge dz_m\\). Its power transport is\n\\(\\eta_0=a(z)^Q\\,dw_1\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge dw_m\\). The trace divided by \\(\\eta_0\\) is\n\\[\n\\tau(f)=\n\\frac{[z_1^{Q-1}\\cdots z_m^{Q-1}](fa(z))}{a(z)^Q}.\n\\]\nFinite duality and the residue transformation rule make this coordinate independent and translation invariant.</p><p>After faithfully flat trivialization by a torsor point \\(b\\), write \\(\\epsilon_i=z_i-b_i\\). Normalized group coordinates \\(u_i\\) have Jacobian determinant \\(a(b)\\), so\n\\[\nu_1^{Q-1}\\cdots u_m^{Q-1}\n=a(b)^{Q-1}\\epsilon_1^{Q-1}\\cdots\\epsilon_m^{Q-1}.\n\\]\nThe displayed functional sends this socle to \\(1\\). It is therefore exactly normalized Hopf integration, with no base-dependent unit. Descent proves equality before trivialization.</p><p>Transitivity gives \\(S^a\\). In the normalized regular-module models, transfer is the quotient \\(\\Lambda_{q'}\\to\\Lambda_q\\), determined by its value on the highest monomial. At \\(t=1\\), invariant forms \\(dy_i/y_i\\) give constant-character projection.</p><p>The residue pairing gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{res}(S(f)h\\eta)\n=\\operatorname{res}(fh^Q\\eta).\n\\]\nThus the transpose is \\(h\\mapsto h^Q\\), with \\(\\eta\\) retained once. Étale trace pairings and flat descent extend this identity to covers, associated modules, and diagrams. The construction is natural for the torsor and frame operations.</p><p>On an affinoid away from \\(x=0\\), a basis norm and intrinsic cover norm differ by a finite factor \\(C\\); rooting actual functions replaces it by \\(C^{1/Q^a}\\to1\\). This consequence concerns the actual Frobenius just identified.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "[Coefficient scope, norm lines, stable lattices, and continuous duality; original Definition 4.1 and Lemmas 4.3–4.6.] Retain \\(1\\leq t<n<p-1\\), the rings of Proposition 2, and the cochain convention of Proposition 3.", "statement": "<p>[Coefficient scope, norm lines, stable lattices, and continuous duality; original Definition 4.1 and Lemmas 4.3–4.6.]</p><p>Retain \\(1\\leq t&lt;n&lt;p-1\\), the rings of Proposition 2, and the cochain convention of Proposition 3.</p><p>An admissible compact coefficient on \\(A\\) or \\(C_1\\) is a finite free module with continuous \\(P_n\\)-action such that, on every successive labeled height stratum and dead-label boundary disc, a finite joint frame ascent and fixed finite Frobenius-root ascent give a finite \\(P_n\\)-stable filtration, split as underlying modules, whose graded pieces are finite sums of the coefficient ring with action only on that ring. A finite translation splitting ascent is also allowed; before splitting, the pieces may be associated to finite translation representations. All filtrations, frames, and splittings have bounded poles on exact-height opens. Commuting auxiliary actions are retained.</p><p>The invariant, Lie, Cartier-dual Lie, torsion-coordinate, conormal, and canonical-line constructions used in the paper, their finite tensor constructions, duals, and power transports, have this property. Reduced-norm character twists are admissible. At sufficiently deep finite levels, the coefficient diagrams admit stable free lattices compatible with transfer and its positive transpose, and compact cohomology has residue and group duality.</p>", "proof": "<p>The permitted geometric coefficients acquire constant frames after the finite markings and splittings specified in the statement. At connected height greater than one, the distribution algebra is local and finite translation representations have trivial-line filtrations; at height one, their characters are summands of finite regular translation representations. Conormal and canonical lines are transported by Proposition 2 and adjunction. Lemmas 4.1–4.4 give the norm twists, stable lattices, and duality assertions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "Norm line", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(\\mathcal G\\) be the algebraic height-\\(s\\), dimension-one group over the characteristic-\\(p\\) universal deformation ring \\(R_s\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(\\mathcal G\\) be the algebraic height-\\(s\\), dimension-one group over the characteristic-\\(p\\) universal deformation ring \\(R_s\\). Then\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{\\det}=\\bigwedge^s\\mathcal G\n\\]\nis a functorial height-one, dimension-one \\(p\\)-divisible group whose marked deformation is canonically constant. The covariant \\(P_s\\)-action is multiplication by the actual unit \\(\\operatorname{Nrd}(g)\\in\\mathbb Z_p^\\times\\).</p><p>With inverse substitution on functions, put\n\\[\n\\mathcal N_s=\\operatorname{Hom}_{R_s}(\\omega_{\\mathcal H_{\\det}},R_s),\n\\qquad\n\\nu_s(g)=\\overline{\\operatorname{Nrd}(g)}\\in\\mathbb F_p^\\times.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\mathcal N_s\\simeq R_s\\otimes_k k(\\nu_s)\n\\]\nequivariantly. Every integral tensor power is admissible on all starting-height and labeled quotients, with the required boundary and root compatibilities.</p>", "proof": "<p>The exterior-power theorem for dimension-at-most-one \\(p\\)-divisible groups, including truncated levels, gives \\(\\mathcal H_{\\det}\\) and its base-change compatibility. Its Cartier dual is étale of height one. Henselian finite étale lifting over \\(R_s\\) uniquely identifies every truncated dual with the constant lift of its special fiber, compatibly with transitions and morphisms. Dualizing gives the canonical marked constancy.</p><p>After splitting the Honda division algebra, the top exterior action is the determinant of the defining \\(s\\)-dimensional representation, hence the reduced norm. Rigidity of the constant height-one lift gives this same action over the whole parameter ring. Inverse substitution acts on invariant differentials by \\(\\overline{\\operatorname{Nrd}(g)}^{-1}\\), so the dual line has character \\(\\nu_s\\).</p><p>On an exact-height stratum, finite connected and étale markings and a finite translation splitting frame \\(\\mathcal G[p]\\). The truncated exterior construction therefore frames \\(\\mathcal H_{\\det}[p]\\). In characteristic \\(p\\), invariant differentials are already those of the first truncated level. This supplies finite-stage frames of \\(\\mathcal N_s\\), not an infinite marking at one stage. Base-change functoriality gives the boundary and root assertions; Frobenius preserves \\(\\nu_s\\), whose values lie in \\(\\mathbb F_p^\\times\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "Corollary 4.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(E\\) be admissible at \\((s,t)\\), and let \\(Q\\) be a finite-dimensional smooth \\(P_s\\)-representation with a finite stable filtration whose quotients are \\(k(\\nu_s^{e_j})\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(E\\) be admissible at \\((s,t)\\), and let \\(Q\\) be a finite-dimensional smooth \\(P_s\\)-representation with a finite stable filtration whose quotients are \\(k(\\nu_s^{e_j})\\). Then \\(E\\otimes_kQ\\) and \\(E\\otimes_kQ^\\vee\\) are admissible. Whenever compact finiteness at \\((s,t)\\) is available, their total \\(P_s\\)-cohomology is finite-dimensional.</p>", "proof": "<p>Tensor the filtration with \\(E\\). Lemma 4.1 identifies each quotient with tensoring by a power of the universal determinant line. Choose one finite stage for the finitely many required frames, and refine the tensor filtration. Its module splittings come from finite-dimensional vector-space splittings. The same construction works on boundaries and after power transport. Dualizing reverses the filtration and inverts the characters. The cohomological assertion is compact finiteness for these admissible coefficients.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.3", "orig": "Lemma 4.5", "depth": 1, "snippet": "At sufficiently deep joint frame level, \\(B_U\\) and every fixed finite associated translation diagram admit \\(P_n\\)-stable free \\(C_1\\)-lattices.", "statement": "<p>At sufficiently deep joint frame level, \\(B_U\\) and every fixed finite associated translation diagram admit \\(P_n\\)-stable free \\(C_1\\)-lattices. Fixed pole strips have finite filtrations by admissible boundary restrictions.</p><p>For stationary functions one can choose a lattice \\(L\\) and \\(b\\) such that\n\\[\nP=x^{-b}L,\\qquad S(P)\\subseteq P,\n\\]\nand every \\(x^{-c}L\\) is carried into \\(P\\) by a high iterate of \\(S\\). In invariant-volume frames the transpose on the dual top-form lattice has Frobenius matrix entries in \\(xC_1\\), and its basis functions are integral over \\(C_1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The leading connected marking is tame and decomposes into invariant-line powers. Subsequent deep normal levels have \\(p\\)-group deck groups, whose regular representations have trivial-line filtrations. Associated descent gives module-split filtrations; use character decomposition at multiplicative height.</p><p>Choose lifted graded bases. Their action matrices are triangular, with uniformly bounded off-diagonal poles by compactness. Successive rescaling by powers of \\(x\\) absorbs those denominators and gives a stable free lattice. Factor \\(x\\) into labels using Proposition 2 to obtain the boundary filtrations.</p><p>Finite Frobenius freeness and a root basis give\n\\[\nS(x^{-c}L)\\subseteq x^{-\\lceil c/Q\\rceil-c_0}L\n\\]\nfor a fixed \\(c_0\\). Choose \\(b\\) above the fixed point of this affine bound. Iteration gives absorption of every fixed pole lattice.</p><p>By Proposition 3 the transpose is actual positive Frobenius. Multiplying its dual lattice by \\(x^b\\) increases Frobenius matrix valuations by \\((Q-1)b\\), up to fixed denominators. Increase \\(b\\) to make all entries divisible by \\(x\\), and to clear the finitely many monic equations of its basis functions. The preceding inequalities persist.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.4", "orig": "Lemma 4.6", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(C\\) be a formal \\(r\\)-disc over \\(k\\), let \\(L\\) be a stable finite free lattice, and put \\( L^*=\\operatorname{Hom}_C(L,C),\\qquad \\omega_C=\\bigwedge^r\\widehat\\Omega^1_{C/k}. \\) With …", "statement": "<p>Let \\(C\\) be a formal \\(r\\)-disc over \\(k\\), let \\(L\\) be a stable finite free lattice, and put\n\\[\nL^*=\\operatorname{Hom}_C(L,C),\\qquad\n\\omega_C=\\bigwedge^r\\widehat\\Omega^1_{C/k}.\n\\]\nWith \\((-)^\\vee=\\operatorname{Hom}_{k,\\mathrm{cts}}(-,k)\\),\n\\[\nL^\\vee\\simeq H^r_{\\mathfrak m_C}(L^*\\otimes_C\\omega_C),\\qquad\nH^i(P_n,L)^\\vee\\simeq H^{n^2-i}(P_n,L^\\vee).\n\\]\nThese identifications preserve finite diagrams and transpose constant cup actions. The groups \\(H^i(P_n,L)\\) are profinite and vanish outside \\(0\\leq i\\leq n^2\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The group \\(P_n\\) has analytic dimension \\(n^2\\) and no \\(p\\)-torsion: an element of order \\(p\\) would embed the degree-\\((p-1)\\) cyclotomic extension in a division algebra of degree \\(n&lt;p-1\\). Compact analytic-group theory supplies a length-\\(n^2\\), finite-projective completed-group-ring resolution. Reduction modulo \\(p\\) is exact. Its orientation is trivial, since conjugation on a split matrix algebra has determinant one.</p><p>The resolution computes compact continuous cohomology by finite quotients and inverse limit, and the bounded-pole bar model on lattice unions. Images for compact lattices are closed. Exact continuous duality gives group duality and cup compatibility.</p><p>In formal coordinates the residue pairing with negative principal monomials identifies the continuous dual: every continuous functional depends on a finite power-series quotient. The residue change-of-variables rule gives coordinate independence and the canonical-line action.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "[Frame quotient, compact supports, and transpose comparison; original Theorem 5.2, Propositions 5.8 and 5.12, and Corollary 5.13.] Fix \\(1\\leq t<n<p-1\\), put \\(m=n-t\\), and retain the frame and coefficient conventions.", "statement": "<p>[Frame quotient, compact supports, and transpose comparison; original Theorem 5.2, Propositions 5.8 and 5.12, and Corollary 5.13.]</p><p>Fix \\(1\\leq t&lt;n&lt;p-1\\), put \\(m=n-t\\), and retain the frame and coefficient conventions. Let \\(C\\) be a complete algebraically closed untilt, \\(E=C^\\flat\\), and choose \\(\\varpi\\in E\\) with \\(\\varpi^\\sharp=p\\). Put\n\\[\nR=(\\mathcal O_E/\\varpi)^a,\\qquad\n\\mathscr A=(\\mathcal O^{\\flat,+}/\\varpi)^a.\n\\]\nAlmostification is with respect to the maximal ideal of \\(\\mathcal O_E\\). Tate twists are retained; over \\(C\\), compatible roots of unity identify them when only frame actions are considered.</p><p>On the Fargues–Fontaine curve, set\n\\[\nV_a=\\mathcal O(1/a),\\qquad\nN_t=\\mathcal Ext^1(V_t,\\mathcal O),\\qquad\nZ=(N_t^m)_{\\mathrm{ind}},\n\\]\nwhere independence means that \\(\\mathbb Q_p^m\\to N_t\\) is injective at every geometric point.</p><p>The perfected full joint frame space \\(\\mathcal X\\) is the determinant-matched space of exact sequences\n\\[\n0\\longrightarrow\\mathcal O^m\\longrightarrow V_n\\longrightarrow V_t\\longrightarrow0,\n\\]\nand \\(\\mathcal X\\to Z\\) is a \\(P_n\\)-torsor. For sufficiently deep finite \\(U\\) and a transfer-stable lattice \\(P\\) of Proposition 4,\n\\[\n\\left(\\operatorname*{colim}_{S^\\vee}R\\Gamma(P_n,P)^\\vee\\right)\\otimes_kR\n\\simeq\nR\\Gamma\\!\\left(U,R\\Gamma_c(Z,\\mathscr A)\\otimes\\langle\\eta\\rangle\\right)[m+n^2].\n\\]\nThis preserves the actual constant \\(P_n\\)-cochain action and transpose frame traces. Consequently\n\\[\n\\bigcap_{r\\geq0}S^rH^i(P_n,P)\n\\]\nis finite-dimensional for every \\(i\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 5.1–5.2 establish the frame quotient. Lemmas 5.3–5.9 compute its supported geometric rows and their finiteness. Lemmas 5.10–5.12 identify the actual Frobenius transpose with those supports, and Lemma 5.13 gives the finite stable transfer image.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Lemma 5.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "After scalar extension to \\(E\\), the completed perfection of the full étale-basis and lift tower, including its boundary, is the product of \\(m\\) open Honda balls.", "statement": "<p>After scalar extension to \\(E\\), the completed perfection of the full étale-basis and lift tower, including its boundary, is the product of \\(m\\) open Honda balls. Its exact-height locus is the locus of independent rational Tate sections. The identification respects \\(P_n\\), integral Tate-basis changes, and finite frame levels.</p>", "proof": "<p>For compatible division coordinates, put\n\\[\nX_{i,l}=z_{i,l}^{p^{nl}},\\qquad X_i=\\lim_lX_{i,l}.\n\\]\nLet \\(\\lambda=\\max_{t\\leq a&lt;n}|x_a|&lt;1\\). The Honda congruence gives\n\\[\n|[p]z-z^{p^n}|\\leq\\lambda|z|^{p^t}.\n\\]\nOn\n\\[\nB_l(\\rho)=\\{\\max_i|z_{i,l}|^{p^{nl}}\\leq\\rho\\},\\qquad\nR_l=\\rho^{p^{-nl}},\n\\]\nthe level-one coordinate ideal and increment estimate give\n\\[\n\\lambda\\leq\n\\max\\{\\rho^{p^{t-n}},\\lambda^{p^t}\\},\n\\]\nhence \\(\\lambda\\leq\\rho^{p^{t-n}}\\). The transition maps preserve these domains and are surjective: a lift with coordinate larger than \\(R_{l+1}\\) would have Honda term dominant and image larger than \\(R_l\\).</p><p>The increments are bounded by \\(\\rho^{p^{nl+t-n}}\\to0\\), proving uniform convergence. A fixed fractional polynomial in the \\(X_i\\) has level approximations with exactly its Gauss norm; surjectivity of domain projections and convergence show the limiting map is isometric. Earlier coordinates are integral series in increasingly divisible powers of later coordinates by Proposition 2; substitution errors tend uniformly to zero, proving density and the completed coordinate identification. The same estimates for addition and frame-change formulas give equivariance.</p><p>A rational relation becomes primitive integral after multiplying by a power of \\(p\\). At a sufficiently high level it is a relation among labeled sections. The full-section polynomial identifies this with étale-rank drop, and conversely rank drop supplies such a relation.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "Theorem 5.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The space \\(\\mathcal X\\) has the \\(P_n\\times U_0\\)-equivariant exact-sequence description in Proposition 5, with determinant comparison a \\(\\mathbb Z_p^\\times\\)-multiple of a fixed comparison.", "statement": "<p>The space \\(\\mathcal X\\) has the \\(P_n\\times U_0\\)-equivariant exact-sequence description in Proposition 5, with determinant comparison a \\(\\mathbb Z_p^\\times\\)-multiple of a fixed comparison. Forgetting the middle frame gives\n\\[\n[\\mathcal X/U/P_n]\\simeq[Z/U].\n\\]\nThe quotient carries\n\\[\n0\\to\\mathcal L_m\\otimes\\mathcal O\\to V_n'\\to V_t'\\to0\n\\]\nwith an integral étale lattice and compact positive-bundle frame reductions. If \\(F/\\mathbb Q_p\\) is unramified of degree \\(t\\), then\n\\[\nN_t\\simeq\\mathbb A_C^{1,\\diamond}/F\n\\]\nwith translation action.</p>", "proof": "<p>The universal-cover comparison sends the independent sections to \\(\\mathcal O^m\\to V_n\\). A saturated image of rank \\(r\\leq m\\) is generated by sections and has degree between \\(0\\) and \\(r/n&lt;1\\), hence degree zero and slope zero. Its rational sections have dimension \\(r\\), so independence forces \\(r=m\\). The quotient is positive of rank \\(t\\), degree one, and therefore of type \\(V_t\\).</p><p>Conversely, an extension of \\(V_t\\) by \\(\\mathcal O^m\\) has no negative quotient. If its middle term is nonbasic, its degree-one classification gives a slope-zero quotient, equivalent to a rational relation among the extension classes. Thus the basic locus is \\(Z\\). Relative slope classification and uniqueness descend these assertions.</p><p>For a connected marking write \\(\\beta=\\alpha^{-1}\\), \\(q=p^t\\), and\n\\[\n\\beta(T)=\\sum b_jT^j,\\qquad [p]_{\\mathcal G}(T)=a_qT^q+\\cdots.\n\\]\nComparing degree \\(qj\\) gives monic equations\n\\[\nb_j^q-a_q^jb_j=P_j(b_1,\\ldots,b_{j-1}),\n\\]\nso all \\(b_j\\) are power bounded and \\(\\beta\\) converges uniformly on strict subballs. It defines a continuous \\(\\mathbb Q_p\\)-linear universal-cover map. It kills Tate torsion on reduced perfectoid tests, hence factors through the positive quotient section sheaf, using sheafified \\(H^1(\\mathcal O)=0\\). Full faithfulness for positive section sheaves gives a bundle map to \\(V_t\\). It is fiberwise nonzero and hence an isomorphism.</p><p>Quotient frames form a \\(D_t^\\times\\)-torsor. Their determinant valuation has \\(P_t\\)-torsor fibers. The valuation of the frame induced by \\(\\alpha\\) descends to a locally constant integer on the independent Honda-ball locus. That locus is geometrically connected: centered Gauss seminorm paths retain the nonzero constant term of every rational-relation function, then join origin Gauss polydiscs. Normalize the determinant comparison so this integer is zero. The marking torsor is then the determinant-zero frame torsor. The same determinant condition cuts middle-frame changes from \\(D_n^\\times\\) to \\(P_n\\).</p><p>Finally the untilt-divisor sequence over the coefficient field \\(F\\),\n\\[\n0\\to\\mathcal O_F(-1)\\to\\mathcal O_F\\to i_*\\mathcal O_C\\to0,\n\\]\nhas section sequence \\(0\\to F\\to\\mathbb A_C^{1,\\diamond}\\to N_t\\to0\\). Finite pushforward identifies \\(\\mathcal O_F(-1)\\) with \\(V_t^\\vee\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.3", "orig": "Finite projective calculation of solid rows", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(K\\) be compact \\(p\\)-adic analytic without \\(p\\)-torsion, and let \\(V\\) be a solid \\(\\mathbb F_p\\)-module with continuous condensed \\(K\\)-action.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(K\\) be compact \\(p\\)-adic analytic without \\(p\\)-torsion, and let \\(V\\) be a solid \\(\\mathbb F_p\\)-module with continuous condensed \\(K\\)-action. For a bounded resolution \\(Q_\\bullet\\to\\mathbb F_p\\) by finitely generated projective \\(\\mathbb F_p[[K]]\\)-modules,\n\\[\nR\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{solid}}(K,V)\n=\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb F_p[[K]]}(Q_\\bullet,V).\n\\]\nPoint evaluation is exact, and every term is a retract of a finite sum of \\(V(*)\\). A commuting scalar on \\(V(*)\\) remains that scalar on point-valued cohomology. For derived coefficients there is the row spectral sequence\n\\[\nH^r_{\\mathrm{solid}}(K;H^s(A))(*)\\Longrightarrow\nH^{r+s}(R\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{solid}}(K;A))(*).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Noetherianity and finite global dimension supply the bounded finite-projective resolution. Condensation is exact and preserves these projectives, and solid modules with \\(K\\)-action are solid completed-group-ring modules. Internal Hom from a finite free module is a finite sum, and projective summands are cut out by idempotents. Evaluation at the point is exact because the point is extremally disconnected projective. Filtering a derived coefficient gives the spectral sequence.</p><p>Geometric finite-coefficient parameter cochains are derived solid: perfectoid analytic rings are inverse limits of discrete vector spaces, products with profinite sets are continuous-function modules, and Artin–Schreier hypercover calculations preserve solidity. Solidification of the free bar on a profinite set gives the free profinite module in degree zero. The solidified augmented bar remains exact with its continuous contraction, and comparison with \\(Q_\\bullet\\) identifies geometric action-nerve descent with the displayed calculation.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.4", "orig": "Lemma 5.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Use compact supports defined by bounded closed subdomains lying overconvergently inside open charts, with strict inner–outer radius gaps.", "statement": "<p>Use compact supports defined by bounded closed subdomains lying overconvergently inside open charts, with strict inner–outer radius gaps. The \\(\\mathscr A\\)-localization and support calculations commute with compact profinite parameters and compact frame torsors. They apply to rational-rank loci and flag incidences in \\(N_t^m\\). On an exact-rank locus the smallest rational plane varies continuously.</p>", "proof": "<p>Affinoid perfectoid almost acyclicity computes integral sections modulo \\(\\varpi\\). In a completed union, any fixed valuation loss is attained at a finite stage by quasicompactness; the same holds for \\(\\varpi\\)-multiples and finite Čech diagrams. Modulo a positive-valuation cutoff, compact-parameter functions are uniformly approximated on finite clopen partitions. Compact torsors are proper and carry compact bounds to common compact bounds.</p><p>A compact frame group has a bounded finite-projective resolution by Lemma 5.3. In the almost solid coefficient category, its invariants are therefore a bounded complex of finite sums and retracts. Thus\n\\[\n\\operatorname*{colim}_K R\\Gamma(G,C_K)\n\\simeq R\\Gamma(G,\\operatorname*{colim}_K C_K).\n\\]\nFor the two torsor presentations of Lemma 5.2, choose common invariant buffered supports upstairs. Their double nerve gives both descents; the two bounded group resolutions commute with the support colimit.</p><p>On a bounded affine lift, cover the rational Grassmannian by finitely many clopen matrix-frame charts. Incidence equations have form\n\\[\nA(L)z=b,\\qquad b\\in F^{m-j}.\n\\]\nUniform bounds put all possible witnesses \\(b\\) in a compact ball. Projection of the resulting closed incidence is proper, with closed generalizing image. Finite meshes and shrinking rational tubes compute this image. At exact rank, the smallest plane is unique, so the proper incidence map has a continuous inverse there.</p><p>The coefficient restriction and localization used here are spatial continuity and the triangle\n\\[\nj!j^*K\\to K\\to i_*i^*K\n\\]\nfor these generalizing closed loci and specializing open complements, not coherent purity for arbitrary characteristic-\\(p\\) boundaries.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.5", "orig": "Lemma 5.5", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For \\(j\\geq0\\), \\( R\\Gamma_c(\\mathbb A_C^{j,\\diamond},\\mathscr A) \\simeq R(-j)[-2j]. \\) Translations and invertible linear changes act trivially, apart from the arithmetic Tate twist.", "statement": "<p>For \\(j\\geq0\\),\n\\[\nR\\Gamma_c(\\mathbb A_C^{j,\\diamond},\\mathscr A)\n\\simeq R(-j)[-2j].\n\\]\nTranslations and invertible linear changes act trivially, apart from the arithmetic Tate twist. The comparison respects compact profinite parameters.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply proper primitive comparison to \\(\\mathbb P_C^j\\) and localization for its hyperplane complement. Restriction preserves hyperplane powers, canceling even degrees \\(0,\\ldots,2j-2\\) and retaining the top affine class in degree \\(2j\\). Naturality gives affine invariance; Lemma 5.4 gives parameter compatibility.</p><p>The boundary germ is the coefficient of the point by shrinking discs and spatial continuity. The paper also verifies this by the Kummer-annulus contraction: shrink Laurent bounds enough to divide nonconstant monomials by their difference operators; the remaining Kummer class vanishes on a smaller disc by a binomial root. This is not a compact-support calculation for a perfected open unit disc.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.6", "orig": "Lemma 5.6", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For \\(j\\geq0\\), \\( R\\Gamma_c(N_t^j,\\mathscr A) \\simeq R(-j)\\otimes\\operatorname{or}_{F^j}^{-1}[-(t+2)j]. \\) The orientation is the top continuous-cohomology line of a lattice in \\(F^j\\).", "statement": "<p>For \\(j\\geq0\\),\n\\[\nR\\Gamma_c(N_t^j,\\mathscr A)\n\\simeq R(-j)\\otimes\\operatorname{or}_{F^j}^{-1}[-(t+2)j].\n\\]\nThe orientation is the top continuous-cohomology line of a lattice in \\(F^j\\). Its \\(GL_j(\\mathbb Z_p)\\)-character is \\((\\det)^{-t}\\). All commuting frame actions and compact parameters are retained.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use \\(N_t^j=\\mathbb A_C^{j,\\diamond}/F^j\\). Put \\(\\Lambda_s=p^{-s}\\mathcal O_F^j\\), and choose open polydiscs \\(D_r\\) with radii between \\(|p|^{-r}\\) and \\(|p|^{-(r+1)}\\). Then \\(F^j\\cap D_r=\\Lambda_r\\), and translates by elements outside \\(\\Lambda_r\\) are disjoint.</p><p>At fixed lattice, descent and Lemma 5.5 give\n\\[\n\\operatorname*{colim}_rR\\Gamma_c(D_r/\\Lambda_s,\\mathscr A)\n\\simeq R\\Gamma(\\Lambda_s,R(-j))[-2j].\n\\]\nThe pairs \\(r\\geq s\\) form the support system; increasing \\(s\\) gives corestriction, summing disjoint translates. The diagonal exhausts \\(N_t^j\\).</p><p>If \\(h=tj\\), lattice cohomology is the exterior algebra on its continuous dual. Corestriction in degree \\(a\\), in homothety frames, multiplies by \\(p^{h-a}\\). The colimit kills all but top degree \\(h\\). Inverse substitution acts on that top line by inverse \\(\\mathbb Q_p\\)-determinant, giving \\((\\det)^{-t}\\).</p><p>The same construction with constant \\(\\mathbb F_p\\)-coefficients gives a natural primitive-comparison equivalence. Its single cohomology line shows that the commuting \\(P_t\\)-action is a smooth finite character, without requiring a \\(P_t\\)-invariant affine chart.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.7", "orig": "Lemma 5.7", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For a rational \\(s\\)-dimensional vector space \\(Q\\), let \\(\\Delta_s=\\{1,\\ldots,s-1\\}\\), and let \\(\\mathrm{Fl}_I(Q)\\) be its compact rational flag space.", "statement": "<p>For a rational \\(s\\)-dimensional vector space \\(Q\\), let \\(\\Delta_s=\\{1,\\ldots,s-1\\}\\), and let \\(\\mathrm{Fl}_I(Q)\\) be its compact rational flag space. For coefficients \\(M\\), the augmented complex\n\\[\n\\mathcal B_Q^a(M)=\n\\bigoplus_{\\substack{I\\subseteq\\Delta_s\\\\ |I|=a-1}}\nLC(\\mathrm{Fl}_I(Q),M),\\qquad1\\leq a\\leq s,\n\\]\nwith signed flag pullbacks, has cohomology only in degree \\(s\\), equal to\n\\[\n\\mathrm{St}(Q;M)=\n\\frac{LC(\\mathrm{Fl}_{\\Delta_s}(Q),M)}\n{\\sum_{i\\in\\Delta_s}LC(\\mathrm{Fl}_{\\Delta_s\\setminus\\{i\\}}(Q),M)}.\n\\]\nFor \\(s=1\\), this is \\(M\\). The assertion is natural and works in locally constant compact-Grassmannian families.</p>", "proof": "<p>Filter partial flag spaces by decreasing Bruhat opens. Restriction gives compactly supported locally constant functions on cells, with surjective extension after compact-open refinement. A flag projection decreases cell length; in equal length it is the same cell-coordinate isomorphism.</p><p>For a permutation \\(w\\), the graded summand is the Boolean complex of subsets containing its right descent set. Toggling a missing descent contracts it unless the descent set is all of \\(\\Delta_s\\). Only the longest permutation survives, in degree \\(s\\), with the stated cokernel. A finite clopen refinement with quotient frames proves the family assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.8", "orig": "Proposition 5.8", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For a rational \\(j\\)-plane \\(W\\subseteq\\mathbb Q_p^m\\), put …", "statement": "<p>For a rational \\(j\\)-plane \\(W\\subseteq\\mathbb Q_p^m\\), put\n\\[\n\\mathfrak o_t(W)=\n\\bigwedge_{\\mathbb F_p}^{tj}\n\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathrm{cts}}\n((W\\cap\\mathbb Z_p^m)\\otimes_{\\mathbb Z_p}\\mathcal O_F,\\mathbb F_p)\n\\otimes_{\\mathbb F_p}R.\n\\]\nFor \\(j&lt;m\\), let \\(\\mathcal V_j\\) be locally constant sections over\n\\(\\mathrm{Gr}_j(\\mathbb Q_p^m)\\) of\n\\[\nW\\longmapsto\\mathfrak o_t(W)(-j)\\otimes_R\n\\mathrm{St}(\\mathbb Q_p^m/W;R),\n\\]\nand put \\(\\mathcal V_m=\\mathfrak o_t(\\mathbb Q_p^m)(-m)\\). Then\n\\[\nH_c^a(Z,\\mathscr A)=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\mathcal V_j,&amp;a=(t+2)j+(m-j),\\quad0\\leq j&lt;m,\\\\\n\\mathcal V_m,&amp;a=(t+2)m,\\\\\n0,&amp;\\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nThe top map to ambient support is an isomorphism. Scalars in \\(1+p\\mathbb Z_p\\) act identically. The actual finite incidence complex and commuting actions are retained.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(\\mathcal I_a\\) parametrize\n\\[\n(z,W_0&lt;\\cdots&lt;W_{a-1}&lt;\\mathbb Q_p^m),\\qquad z\\in N_t(W_0).\n\\]\nTheir projections are proper by Lemma 5.4. The alternating complex\n\\[\n\\mathscr A_{N_t^m}\\to\\pi_{1*}\\mathscr A_{\\mathcal I_1}\n\\to\\cdots\\to\\pi_{m*}\\mathscr A_{\\mathcal I_m}\n\\]\nrepresents extension by zero from \\(Z\\). At a dependent point, adjoining the smallest supporting plane contracts the augmented nerve. Lemma 5.4 makes this a contraction on exact-rank tube and parameter complexes, not merely on geometric stalk ranks.</p><p>Compute plane-fiber supports using Lemma 5.6, then quotient flags using Lemma 5.7. The only entries are\n\\[\nE_2^{m-j,(t+2)j}=\\mathcal V_j\\quad(j&lt;m),\\qquad\nE_2^{0,(t+2)m}=\\mathcal V_m.\n\\]\nA further differential would require simultaneously\n\\(r=j-j'\\) and \\(r-1=(t+2)(j-j')\\), impossible. Total degrees are distinct. The ambient projection gives the top identification.</p><p>Scalars fix rational flags and act on a \\(j\\)-plane orientation by their reduction to exponent \\(-tj\\), which is one on \\(1+p\\mathbb Z_p\\). Every construction is a natural support, restriction, pullback, or lattice-corestriction map.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.9", "orig": "Corollary 5.9", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For compact open \\(U\\subseteq U_0\\) and a fixed finite character \\(\\chi\\), \\( H^a\\!\\left(U,R\\Gamma_c(Z,\\mathscr A)\\otimes\\chi\\right) \\) vanishes outside a finite range and has bounded almost length over \\(R\\).", "statement": "<p>For compact open \\(U\\subseteq U_0\\) and a fixed finite character \\(\\chi\\),\n\\[\nH^a\\!\\left(U,R\\Gamma_c(Z,\\mathscr A)\\otimes\\chi\\right)\n\\]\nvanishes outside a finite range and has bounded almost length over \\(R\\). If such a group is \\(V\\otimes_kR\\), then \\(V\\) is finite-dimensional.</p>", "proof": "<p>Keep the finite flag resolutions. Shapiro reduces the matrix direction to compact parabolic groups with line coefficients; an open subgroup has finitely many flag orbits. Include the compact connected-frame direction. These groups have no \\(p\\)-torsion and admit bounded finite-projective resolutions. Their smooth line twists become trivial on an open subgroup. Thus the computing complexes have finitely many finite almost-free terms.</p><p>Normalize the length of \\(R\\) to one. Finite extensions and subquotients have the corresponding bounded length. Every \\(r\\)-dimensional subspace of \\(V\\) contributes length \\(r\\) after faithful scalar extension, so \\(r\\) is bounded.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.10", "orig": "Lemma 5.10", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Choose \\(P=x^{-b}L\\) as in Proposition 4, and put \\( M=\\operatorname{Hom}_{C_1}(P,\\omega_{C_1}),\\qquad P^\\vee=H^m_{\\mathfrak m}(M). \\) In volume frames write \\(e_i=f_i\\eta\\), where \\(f_i\\) are integral finite-cover …", "statement": "<p>Choose \\(P=x^{-b}L\\) as in Proposition 4, and put\n\\[\nM=\\operatorname{Hom}_{C_1}(P,\\omega_{C_1}),\\qquad\nP^\\vee=H^m_{\\mathfrak m}(M).\n\\]\nIn volume frames write \\(e_i=f_i\\eta\\), where \\(f_i\\) are integral finite-cover functions, and\n\\[\nF(e_i)=f_i^Q\\eta=\\sum_j a_{ji}e_j,\\qquad a_{ji}\\in xC_1.\n\\]\nSet \\(C^{(r)}=C_1^{1/Q^r}\\), \\(M_r=\\bigoplus_iC^{(r)}e_i^{(r)}\\), with transitions\n\\[\ne_i^{(r)}\\longmapsto\n\\sum_j a_{ji}^{1/Q^{r+1}}e_j^{(r+1)}.\n\\]\nLet \\(\\mathscr M_r\\) be the corresponding free integral analytic lattice on the perfected open \\(m\\)-disc. Then\n\\[\n\\left(\\operatorname*{colim}_F H^m_{\\mathfrak m}(M)\\right)\\otimes_kR[-m]\n\\simeq\nR\\Gamma_c\\!\\left(\\mathbb D^{m,\\mathrm{perf}},\n\\operatorname*{colim}_r\\mathscr M_r\\right),\n\\]\nnaturally for lattice maps, integral coordinates, and continuous \\(P_n\\)-parameters.</p>", "proof": "<p>Power renaming \\(\\theta_r(ce_i^{(r)})=c^{Q^r}e_i\\) identifies the displayed transitions with \\(F=S^\\vee\\), also on Čech fractions.</p><p>For \\(0&lt;a&lt;b&lt;c&lt;s&lt;1\\), compare genuine support in the closed radius-\\(a\\) disc with the fiber of restriction from the radius-\\(s\\) disc to the Laurent exterior \\(\\bigcup_i\\{b\\leq|w_i|\\leq s\\}\\). Excision gives maps from radius-\\(a\\) support to this Laurent fiber and then to radius-\\(c\\) support. The buffered and genuine systems interleave cofinally, with forward maps enlarging supports.</p><p>Affinoid almost acyclicity computes the Laurent fiber by a finite augmented Čech complex. The monomial contraction preserves Gauss weights and kills every exponent vector except those negative in all coordinates, which survive in degree \\(m\\). Modulo any fixed positive-valuation cutoff only finitely many terms remain. As the inner radius tends to one, each fixed negative monomial has coefficient quotient almost \\(R\\). This gives discrete finite-sum principal parts tensored with \\(R\\).</p><p>Separate module-root and scalar-root indices. Every finite principal part has a common root denominator, so the diagonal is cofinal; \\(\\theta_{r+1}\\iota_r=F\\theta_r\\) gives the claimed system. The comparison is the algebraic Čech-fraction map, allowing arbitrarily small losses before almostification. Residue transformation gives coordinate naturality. Uniform compact-parameter approximation gives the same calculation on parameter complexes.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.11", "orig": "Lemma 5.11", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(j:\\mathbb D^\\times\\hookrightarrow\\mathbb D^{m,\\mathrm{perf}}\\) be \\(x\\neq0\\), and let \\(\\nu:\\mathcal X/U\\to\\mathbb D^\\times\\) be the finite analytically realized frame cover.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(j:\\mathbb D^\\times\\hookrightarrow\\mathbb D^{m,\\mathrm{perf}}\\) be \\(x\\neq0\\), and let\n\\(\\nu:\\mathcal X/U\\to\\mathbb D^\\times\\) be the finite analytically realized frame cover. Then\n\\[\n\\operatorname*{colim}_r\\mathscr M_r\n\\simeq\nj!\\nu_*\\mathscr A_{\\mathcal X/U}\\otimes\\langle\\eta\\rangle\n\\]\nalmost, compatibly with frame trace, frame change, \\(P_n\\), and the volume character.</p>", "proof": "<p>Away from \\(x=0\\), the basis norm and intrinsic integral cover norm differ by a fixed two-sided factor \\(C_0\\). At root stage \\(r\\) it is at most \\(C_0^{1/Q^r}\\). Integral basis functions give the actual lattice map, and arbitrarily small eventual norm losses prove almost surjectivity and, on \\(\\varpi\\)-multiples, almost injectivity. Finite fractional-polynomial approximation handles completion.</p><p>On \\(x=0\\), every transition is zero because its entries are divisible by a root of \\(x\\). Equivalently, an element of a fixed stage vanishes modulo \\(\\varpi\\) on a sufficiently small tube after its next transition. No common tube for all stages is required. Continuity and open–closed localization identify the limit with extension by zero.</p><p>These are the actual cover functions and actual Frobenius maps from Proposition 3; norm estimates prove equivalence without replacing their actions. Compact parameter families have common finite bases and pole bounds.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.12", "orig": "Proposition 5.12", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For the stated \\(P,U\\), \\( \\left(\\operatorname*{colim}_{S^\\vee}R\\Gamma(P_n,P)^\\vee\\right)\\otimes_kR \\simeq R\\Gamma\\!\\left(U,R\\Gamma_c(Z,\\mathscr A)\\otimes\\langle\\eta\\rangle\\right)[m+n^2]. \\) In degree \\(-i\\) …", "statement": "<p>For the stated \\(P,U\\),\n\\[\n\\left(\\operatorname*{colim}_{S^\\vee}R\\Gamma(P_n,P)^\\vee\\right)\\otimes_kR\n\\simeq\nR\\Gamma\\!\\left(U,R\\Gamma_c(Z,\\mathscr A)\\otimes\\langle\\eta\\rangle\\right)[m+n^2].\n\\]\nIn degree \\(-i\\),\n\\[\n\\left(\\operatorname*{colim}_{S^\\vee}H^i(P_n,P)^\\vee\\right)\\otimes_kR\n\\simeq\nH^{m+n^2-i}\\!\\left(U,R\\Gamma_c(Z,\\mathscr A)\\otimes\\langle\\eta\\rangle\\right).\n\\]\nThe comparison is linear for actual constant \\(\\mathbb F_p\\)-cochains, with dual signs, and transpose frame trace is geometric pullback.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply group and residue duality from Proposition 4:\n\\[\nR\\Gamma(P_n,P)^\\vee\\simeq R\\Gamma(P_n,P^\\vee)[n^2],\n\\qquad P^\\vee=H^m_{\\mathfrak m}(M).\n\\]\nThe bounded finite resolution commutes with the root colimit. Lemmas 5.10–5.11 identify this with\n\\[\nR\\Gamma(P_n,R\\Gamma_c(\\mathcal X/U,\\mathscr A)\\otimes\\langle\\eta\\rangle)[m+n^2].\n\\]\nThe common torsor double nerve of Lemmas 5.2 and 5.4 gives the displayed right side.</p><p>Constant cochains act by multiplying the coefficient unit on clopen pieces of the \\(P_n\\)-nerve, equivalently by the structural pullback from \\(BP_n\\). Group duality transposes this action with graded signs. Residue pairings, supported Čech maps, Frobenius comparison, and the common double nerve preserve that same structural action. Étale trace and its transpose respect the residue pairing, giving the frame-map direction.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.13", "orig": "Corollary 5.13", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every \\(i\\), \\( \\dim_k\\bigcap_{r\\geq0}S^rH^i(P_n,P)<\\infty. \\)", "statement": "<p>For every \\(i\\),\n\\[\n\\dim_k\\bigcap_{r\\geq0}S^rH^i(P_n,P)&lt;\\infty.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 5.9 and 5.12 make the dual direct limit finite-dimensional. Its compact dual is the inverse limit with transition \\(S\\). Projection from this inverse limit has image exactly the intersection of transfer images: nested compact sets supply compatible preimages. The intersection is therefore a quotient of a finite-dimensional vector space.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Compact coefficient finiteness", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Fix \\(1\\leq t<n<p-1\\).", "statement": "<p>Fix \\(1\\leq t&lt;n&lt;p-1\\). Assume compact finiteness for admissible coefficients on every dead-label disc at the higher starting-height pairs \\((n,s)\\), \\(s&gt;t\\), including finite root transports; at \\(t=n\\), the initial case is finite-coefficient cohomology on the zero-dimensional disc.</p><p>Then every admissible compact coefficient on \\(A\\) or \\(C_1\\) has finite total continuous \\(P_n\\)-cohomology. Every finite-frame localized coefficient, and the localization of every admissible compact coefficient, has countable-dimensional \\(P_n\\)-cohomology.</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose first \\(t&gt;1\\). If countability fails at arbitrarily deep levels, choose \\(b_0\\) as in Lemma 6.3. At deep levels, Lemma 6.1 and the augmentation argument give\n\\[\nI_X=\\bigcap_{a\\geq0}\\operatorname{im}(S^aM^{b_0}\\to X^{b_0})\n\\]\nfinite. Fix one frame level satisfying Lemma 6.4 and the Koszul edge.</p><p>Choose a free source lattice \\(L_s\\) in the coefficient associated to \\(N_s\\). Modulo a countable-dimensional contribution, source classes come from \\(x^aL_s\\) for arbitrarily large fixed \\(a\\), by the pole-strip argument. At growing divisible torsion levels distinguish\n\\[\nQ_r=p^{t\\ell_r},\\qquad q_r=p^{t\\ell_r/(t-1)}.\n\\]\nThere are fixed denominator losses \\(c_{\\mathrm{in}},c_{\\mathrm{out}}\\) for the source inclusion and fixed boundary diagram. Choose \\(a&gt;c_{\\mathrm{in}}\\). After powering, the output order is at least\n\\[\nQ_r(a-c_{\\mathrm{in}})-c_{\\mathrm{out}}\\longrightarrow\\infty.\n\\]\nProposition 3 applies to the entire finite diagram. The last transfer is an arbitrarily high \\(S\\)-iterate. Therefore, for every \\(a_0\\), a sufficiently large \\(r\\) gives\n\\[\nc_{q_r}e_{s,*}[z]=\\gamma_{q_r,*}[z]\n\\in\\operatorname{im}(S^{a_0}M^{b_0}\\to X^{b_0}).\n\\]\nThe equality holds at the one fixed frame level, and its scalar is nonzero. Thus the positive-lattice image lies in \\(I_X\\). The whole edge image is countable, contradicting its epimorphism modulo countable spaces. Hence every \\(X^i\\) is countable at sufficiently deep levels.</p><p>At \\(t=1\\), use exact character decomposition. Constants split at every root step, and stationary coordinates give\n\\[\nS^r\\Phi_{Q^r}=1,\\qquad \\Phi_{Q^r}(f)=f^{Q^r}.\n\\]\nThe same positive-lattice estimate places a positive representative in arbitrarily high transfer images; the retraction identifies it with the original compact class. Proposition 5 bounds this intersection, and pole strips leave only a countable remainder.</p><p>Countability descends from deep frames by finite flat Čech descent. At normal levels the terms are finite products indexed by deck-group powers, with \\(P_n\\) fixing that index set. Admissible coefficients reduce by their filtrations to functions. Finite translation splittings descend by their bar constructions; at height one characters are summands of finite root rings, and power transport reduces them to the established case. In each total degree only finitely many bar terms contribute.</p><p>For compact admissible \\(E\\) on \\(C_1\\), the map to \\(E[1/x]\\) has countable kernel by higher-height pole strips, and its target cohomology is countable. Its source cohomology is profinite by Proposition 4, hence finite by Baire. It vanishes outside a bounded range. On \\(A\\), first pass to the labeled exact-height locus, descend countability, and use strips for \\(x_t\\), whose quotients lie on the next starting-height disc. Root transport carries all coefficient and action data together.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "Lemma 6.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(M\\) be a compact \\(k\\)-vector space, \\(k\\) finite, and \\(S:M\\to M\\) continuous linear.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(M\\) be a compact \\(k\\)-vector space, \\(k\\) finite, and \\(S:M\\to M\\) continuous linear. Suppose \\(M/SM\\) and\n\\(I=\\bigcap_{a\\geq0}S^aM\\) are finite-dimensional. Then \\(S(I)=I\\), \\(S|_I\\) is invertible, and \\(M/I\\) is finitely generated over \\(k[[z]]\\), with \\(z=S\\). In particular \\(S\\) has finite kernel and cokernel.</p><p>If \\(f:M\\to X\\) commutes with \\(S\\) and \\(\\ker f\\) is locally \\(S\\)-nilpotent, then \\(\\ker f\\) is finite and\n\\[\n\\bigcap_{a\\geq0}\\operatorname{im}(S^aM\\to X)=f(I)\n\\]\nis finite.</p>", "proof": "<p>Nested compact sets \\(S^{-1}(y)\\cap S^aM\\) give \\(S(I)=I\\); finiteness gives invertibility. The images \\(S^a(M/I)\\) shrink uniformly to zero, since otherwise a compact set outside a zero neighborhood would meet their zero intersection.</p><p>Lift a finite basis modulo \\(S\\), repeatedly subtract its combinations and divide the remainder by \\(S\\). Uniform convergence gives a continuous surjection \\(k[[z]]^r\\to M/I\\). The kernel and cokernel of \\(z\\) on a finite module over this discrete valuation ring are finite-dimensional.</p><p>The image of \\(\\ker f\\) in \\(M/I\\) lies in its finite \\(z\\)-power torsion. Thus \\(\\ker f\\) is finite and closed. A fiber meeting every \\(S^aM\\) meets their intersection by compactness, proving the final equality.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.2", "orig": "Lemma 6.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assume \\(t>1\\), set \\(d=m(t-1)\\), and use \\(\\Lambda=k[[D_1,\\ldots,D_d]]\\).", "statement": "<p>Assume \\(t&gt;1\\), set \\(d=m(t-1)\\), and use \\(\\Lambda=k[[D_1,\\ldots,D_d]]\\). Let \\(\\mathcal V\\) be the Serre quotient of \\(k\\)-vector spaces by countable-dimensional spaces, and let \\(\\mathcal G\\) be the category of germs of sequences in \\(\\mathcal V\\), along a cofinal sequence of principal joint frame levels.</p><p>For a finite nilpotent \\(\\Lambda\\)-module \\(N\\), let \\(T^i(N)\\) be the associated-coefficient \\(P_n\\)-cohomology sequence in \\(\\mathcal G\\). Extend levelwise to pro-objects, and write \\(\\overline T^i(\\Lambda)\\) for the inverse regular-module system.</p><p>Then \\(\\overline T^i(\\Lambda)\\) is constant, its augmentation to \\(T^i(k)\\) is monic, and its \\(\\Lambda\\)-action factors through \\(k\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Fixed pole strips have finite cohomology by the assumed higher-height compact assertions: factor \\(x\\) into labels and filter by boundary restrictions and conormal powers. Truncation supplies continuous sections. Thus, with\n\\[\nM^i=H^i(P_n,P),\\qquad X^i=H^i(P_n,B_U),\n\\]\nthe comparison has countable kernel and cokernel. Its kernel is locally \\(S\\)-nilpotent, since a primitive at one pole bound eventually transfers into \\(P\\). Proposition 5 gives a finite stable transfer intersection.</p><p>Proposition 3 identifies the inverse regular-module system with the stationary transfer system on \\(X^i\\). Completed Koszul resolutions are pro-exact by adic Artin–Rees. Constants in a pro-abelian category are closed under kernels, cokernels, and extensions.</p><p>Induct downward in \\(i\\) above the uniform \\(n^2\\)-bound. Resolve the augmentation ideal by the truncated completed Koszul resolution. The long exact sequences, and the induction in larger degrees, make the image of\n\\(\\overline T^i(\\Lambda)\\to T^i(k)\\) constant. Its decreasing-image presentation \\(S^aX^i\\) stabilizes at some \\(h\\) in \\(\\mathcal G\\).</p><p>At sufficiently deep levels, put \\(M=S^hM^i\\). Then \\(M/SM\\) is countable and compact, hence finite: a countable compact Hausdorff group is finite by Baire. Its stable part is finite by Proposition 5. Lemma 6.1 makes \\(S\\) have finite kernel and cokernel on \\(M\\), hence countable kernel and cokernel on the stabilized \\(S^hX^i\\). Thus \\(S\\) is an isomorphism there in \\(\\mathcal G\\), proving constancy and monicity. The augmentation is \\(\\Lambda\\)-linear and its target has trivial augmentation-ideal action, so the same holds on the source.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.3", "orig": "Fixed Koszul edge", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Under the assumptions of Lemma 6.2, suppose some \\(T^i(k)\\neq0\\), and let \\(b_0\\) be the largest such degree.", "statement": "<p>Under the assumptions of Lemma 6.2, suppose some \\(T^i(k)\\neq0\\), and let \\(b_0\\) be the largest such degree. For some sufficiently large allowed distribution exponent \\(s\\), a generator\n\\[\ne_s\\in\\operatorname{Ext}_\\Lambda^d(N_s,k),\\qquad\nN_s=\\Lambda/(D_1^s,\\ldots,D_d^s),\n\\]\ninduces an epimorphism\n\\[\nT^{b_0-d}(N_s)\\longrightarrow T^{b_0}(k)\n\\]\nin \\(\\mathcal G\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The augmentation sequence and maximality of \\(b_0\\) make\n\\(\\overline T^{b_0}(\\Lambda)\\to T^{b_0}(k)\\) an isomorphism. Apply the cochain functor to\n\\(K_s=K(D_1^s,\\ldots,D_d^s)\\) in columns \\([-d,0]\\). Its vertical page consists of \\(\\overline T^j(\\Lambda)\\), with zero first differential by Lemma 6.2.</p><p>For \\(s'&gt;s\\), the upward regular-module inclusion is lifted by\n\\[\ne_I\\longmapsto\n\\left(\\prod_{j\\notin I}D_j^{s'-s}\\right)e_I.\n\\]\nIt is the identity in column \\(-d\\) and augmentation-ideal valued elsewhere. There are no incoming differentials at \\((-d,b_0)\\). Increase \\(s\\) successively for the finitely many possible outgoing differentials; naturality kills each because the corresponding target comparison is zero. The entire entry survives as the quotient edge, represented by the generator \\(e_s\\).</p><p>Completed resolutions, torsion-module Ext, and finite Yoneda extensions give the same operation. Injective envelopes of torsion modules remain torsion; finite generators can be lifted successively through a finite extension and its finite comparison diagrams to replace them by finite-length diagrams.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.4", "orig": "Uniform restriction for fixed endpoints", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Choose finite extension diagrams for \\(e_s\\) and for nonzero \\(e\\in\\operatorname{Ext}_\\Lambda^d(k,k)\\), equivariant for a compact open frame group \\(U\\).", "statement": "<p>Choose finite extension diagrams for \\(e_s\\) and for nonzero \\(e\\in\\operatorname{Ext}_\\Lambda^d(k,k)\\), equivariant for a compact open frame group \\(U\\). For every allowed \\(q\\geq s\\), form\n\\[\n\\gamma_q:\nN_s\\longrightarrow N_q\n\\xrightarrow{\\operatorname{coind}(e)}N_q[d]\n\\longrightarrow k[d],\n\\]\nusing upward inclusion and normalized transfer.</p><p>There is one open \\(U'\\subseteq U\\), independent of \\(q\\), on which\n\\[\n\\gamma_q=c_qe_s,\\qquad c_q\\in k^\\times,\n\\]\nas equivariant extensions.</p>", "proof": "<p>Without auxiliary actions, coinduction is the base change \\(D_i\\mapsto D_i^q\\) of the fundamental Koszul class. Its top coefficient is a unit; the comparison from \\(K_s\\) is identity in the leftmost column. Hence the equality holds in ordinary Ext.</p><p>All differences lie in the fixed-endpoint forgetful kernel\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ext}_{\\mathcal T\\rtimes U}^d(N_s,k)\n\\longrightarrow\\operatorname{Ext}_\\Lambda^d(N_s,k).\n\\]\nAfter shrinking to pro-\\(p\\) analytic \\(U\\), the composition-of-invariants spectral sequence has terms\n\\[\nH^a(U,\\operatorname{Ext}_\\Lambda^j(N_s,k)),\\qquad\n\\dim_k\\operatorname{Ext}_\\Lambda^j(N_s,k)=\\binom dj.\n\\]\nAnalytic-group finiteness makes the total Ext, hence the kernel, a finite set.</p><p>These classes have finite equivariant Yoneda representatives: finite orbit sets have finite-dimensional \\(\\Lambda\\)-spans. An underlying zero-comparison becomes equivariant after shrinking to fix the finite quotient of \\(\\Lambda\\) and all finite objects, and assigning trivial action to its added comparison objects. Thus each kernel class vanishes on an open subgroup. Intersect over the finite kernel. This single subgroup works for all \\(q\\); no common trivialization of the growing \\(N_q\\) is used.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Removal of the pole bound", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Fix \\(1\\leq t<n<p-1\\).", "statement": "<p>Fix \\(1\\leq t&lt;n&lt;p-1\\). Assume:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Compact finiteness at the current pair \\((n,t)\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. At every \\((n,s)\\), \\(s&gt;t\\), finite full-connected, fixed-matrix-level cohomology, a uniform open scalar subgroup acting identically after full-frame perfection, and finite individual matrix-cohomology rows with \\(P_s\\)-filtrations by powers of \\(\\nu_s\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Compact finiteness for admissible coefficients at all smaller-total-height pairs \\((s,t)\\), \\(s&lt;n\\).</p><p>Then, for every \\(e\\in\\mathbb Z\\),\n\\[\nH^*(P_n,C_1[1/x]\\otimes\\omega^e)\n\\]\nhas finite total dimension, where \\(\\omega\\) is the invariant cotangent line. Consequently every finite joint frame coefficient \\(B_U\\) has finite total bounded-pole \\(P_n\\)-cohomology.</p><p>All full-frame and perfected unions in these hypotheses are algebraic and use common finite stages and pole bounds.</p>", "proof": "<p>Filter support at \\(x=x_t\\) by\n\\[\nJ_s=(x_t,\\ldots,x_{s-1}),\\qquad t&lt;s\\leq n.\n\\]\nThe successive terms are\n\\[\nH^{s-t}_{J_s}(C_1\\otimes\\omega^e)[1/x_s][-(s-t)],\n\\]\nsince the transverse sequence is regular. Finite Čech or Koszul fractions and truncation sections preserve bounded-pole cochains.</p><p>For \\(s=n\\), residue and orientable \\(P_n\\)-duality identify the dual row with compact cohomology of the finite dual canonical-line coefficient. It is admissible by Proposition 4, so current compact finiteness applies.</p><p>For \\(t&lt;s&lt;n\\), use Lemmas 7.1–7.3. After \\(P_n\\)-cohomology and deep translation invariants, take a sufficiently small surviving matrix group. Lemma 7.4, the assumed full-connected finiteness, and étale descent give finite actual rows\n\\[\nQ=H^a(V,H^i(P_n,B_{\\mathrm{full},s}^{\\mathrm{perf}})).\n\\]\nBy hypothesis these have \\(P_s\\)-filtrations by norm powers. The resulting terms are\n\\[\nQ\\otimes_kH^r_{\\mathfrak m'}(M\\otimes_{R'}F),\n\\]\nwhere \\(F\\) is a finite Lie, invariant-line, or torsion-coordinate construction.</p><p>Residue and \\(P_s\\)-duality give\n\\[\nH^b(P_s,Q\\otimes H^r_{\\mathfrak m'}(M\\otimes F))^\\vee\n\\simeq\nH^{s^2-b}(P_s,Q^\\vee\\otimes L_{M,F}),\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nL_{M,F}=\\operatorname{Hom}_{R'}(M\\otimes F,\\omega_{R'}).\n\\]\nThis is admissible at the smaller pair \\((s,t)\\): finite markings and splittings frame the torsion and Lie constructions on every boundary; finite duality and conormal adjunction supply the canonical twist. Proposition 4 makes its norm-character twists admissible. The assumed smaller compact finiteness and the finite filtration of \\(Q^\\vee\\) therefore make the displayed group finite.</p><p>Forget the remaining finite translation quotient by its augmented bar. Each degree adds only finite universal torsion-coordinate factors to \\(F\\), leaving the special coefficient defining \\(Q\\) unchanged. The same calculation applies degreewise; finitely many degrees contribute to each total degree. Finite label and matrix quotients are handled identically. The intrinsic \\(P_n\\)-bound makes total cohomology finite.</p><p>All support rows are thus finite. Current compact finiteness and\n\\[\nR\\Gamma_{(x)}(C_1\\otimes\\omega^e)\\to\nC_1\\otimes\\omega^e\\to C_1[1/x]\\otimes\\omega^e\n\\]\ngive bounded-pole finiteness. The tame marking decomposes into invariant-line powers; deeper normal levels have \\(p\\)-group regular filtrations. This gives deep finite-frame finiteness, and finite torsor descent gives every \\(U\\). Every construction is made at common Artin thickness, finite stage, and pole bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "Relative Artin splitting, including the marked-deformation construction of Section 7.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For \\(t<s<n\\), put \\(r=s-t\\), \\(e_s=n-s\\), and \\( R'=k[[v_t,\\ldots,v_{s-1}]],\\qquad \\mathfrak m'=(v_t,\\ldots,v_{s-1}),\\qquad R_N=R'/\\mathfrak m'^N. \\) Mark the reduced height-\\(s\\) connected group and surviving étale …", "statement": "<p>For \\(t&lt;s&lt;n\\), put \\(r=s-t\\), \\(e_s=n-s\\), and\n\\[\nR'=k[[v_t,\\ldots,v_{s-1}]],\\qquad\n\\mathfrak m'=(v_t,\\ldots,v_{s-1}),\\qquad R_N=R'/\\mathfrak m'^N.\n\\]\nMark the reduced height-\\(s\\) connected group and surviving étale Tate basis, lift finite marking data across a nilpotent neighborhood, and adjoin compatible lifts of the surviving basis. Let \\(E_N\\) be the resulting algebraic union before adjoining \\(C_1\\). Then \\(E_N\\) is flat over \\(R_N\\), its special fiber\n\\[\nS_s=E_N/\\mathfrak m'E_N=B_{\\mathrm{full},s}^{\\mathrm{perf}}\n\\]\nis perfect, and there is a canonical isomorphism\n\\[\nR_N\\otimes_kS_s\\xrightarrow{\\simeq}E_N.\n\\]\nIt respects restriction in \\(N\\), \\(P_n\\), \\(P_s\\), surviving matrix actions, and bounded-pole compact-parameter cochains. The translation group remains\n\\[\n\\mathcal T'=(T_p\\mathcal G)^{e_s}\n\\]\nover the universal smaller deformation \\(R'\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>At each square-zero step \\(J=I^a/I^{a+1}\\), use trivial nilpotent divided powers. Display lifting classifies marked formal-group lifts by Hodge-filtration lifts, a torsor under \\(J^{s-1}\\). Universal parameter lifts form the same torsor by Kodaira–Spencer. Affineness removes gluing obstruction; display full faithfulness and rigidity under nilpotent reduction give the actual marked isomorphism and uniqueness. The height conditions set parameters below \\(t\\) to zero. At fixed nilpotence order, each specified finite coefficient diagram descends to one finite marking stage; this does not assert a finite stage for the whole infinite isomorphism.</p><p>Orient the isomorphism by\n\\[\nh[p]_{\\mathrm{original}}=[p]_{\\mathcal G}h,\\qquad c=h'(0).\n\\]\nSuccessive height comparisons give, on conormals,\n\\[\nx_i\\equiv c^{p^i-1}v_i+\\sum_{j&lt;i}b_{ij}v_j\\pmod{J_s^2}.\n\\]\nThe triangular diagonal is invertible, so the transverse ideals and their powers agree. Before marking the graded algebra is\n\\[\n(A/J_s)[1/x_s][X_t,\\ldots,X_{s-1}]/(X)^N.\n\\]\nFinite étale marking base change preserves it; the triangular comparison identifies it with the \\(R_N\\)-graded algebra. A \\(k\\)-linear lift of the special fiber extends to an \\(R_N\\)-linear map that is a graded isomorphism, hence a module isomorphism. Finite locally free lift torsors and their union preserve flatness.</p><p>For a flat Artin \\(R\\)-algebra \\(E\\) with perfect special fiber \\(S\\), choose \\(q=p^a\\) killing the nilpotent ideal and fixing \\(k\\), and define\n\\[\n\\sigma(z)=\\widetilde{z^{1/q}}^{\\,q}.\n\\]\nIt is independent of the lift and of sufficiently large \\(q\\), is a functorial \\(k\\)-algebra section, and flatness makes \\(R\\otimes_kS\\to E\\) a graded, hence actual, isomorphism. Apply this to \\(E_N\\). Functoriality gives the actions; \\(P_n\\) acts only on \\(S_s\\), while \\(P_s\\) acts on both factors.</p><p>Transverse truncation, finite-projective idempotents, monomial division, and finite torsion-coordinate bases give continuous additive sections and parameter extraction. Their finitely many structure constants and inverse triangular matrices have common denominators. The section formula moves to one further finite root stage. Thus the isomorphism and inverse preserve common-stage bounded-pole cochains.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "Full sections on a split kernel", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Put \\( H_c=\\mathcal G[p]/\\ker(F^t),\\qquad \\operatorname{rank}(H_c)=p^{s-t}. \\) After Lemma 7.1’s lift ascent, the pullback of \\(C_1\\) is a finite disjoint union of coefficients from \\(R'\\) of the form …", "statement": "<p>Put\n\\[\nH_c=\\mathcal G[p]/\\ker(F^t),\\qquad \\operatorname{rank}(H_c)=p^{s-t}.\n\\]\nAfter Lemma 7.1’s lift ascent, the pullback of \\(C_1\\) is a finite disjoint union of coefficients from \\(R'\\) of the form\n\\[\nM=C_1^{(s,t)}\\otimes_{R'}\\mathcal O(H_c)^{\\otimes e_s}.\n\\]\nIt is finite free over \\(R'\\), free over \\(C_1^{(s,t)}\\) with finite torsion-coordinate factors. This holds over arbitrary nilpotent test rings, with the stated invariant-line twists. A sufficiently small surviving matrix congruence subgroup fixes components and factors; translations use a finite torsion level.</p><p>Moreover,\n\\[\nH^r_{\\mathfrak m'}(M)=\n\\operatorname*{colim}_N\nM/(v_t^N,\\ldots,v_{s-1}^N)M\n\\]\nwith multiplication transition \\(v_t\\cdots v_{s-1}\\), and the split local row is\n\\[\nS_s\\otimes_kH^r_{\\mathfrak m'}(M).\n\\]\nIts \\(P_n\\)-cohomology is\n\\[\nH^i(P_n,S_s)\\otimes_kH^r_{\\mathfrak m'}(M).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Fullness is the intrinsic norm identity\n\\[\n\\det(m_f:\\mathcal O(H)\\to\\mathcal O(H))\n=\\prod_{v\\in\\mathbb F_p^{n-t}}f(\\ell(v))\n\\]\nafter arbitrary base change. Proposition 2 identifies this algebra with \\(C_1\\).</p><p>On the split tower \\(H=H_c\\times\\mathbb F_p^{e_s}\\). A full tuple projects onto the étale group: an omitted component contradicts the norm identity for its zero–one indicator function. Decompose by the finite set of surjections\n\\(\\mathbb F_p^{n-t}\\twoheadrightarrow\\mathbb F_p^{e_s}\\). Choose kernel and complement. A tuple is then a kernel homomorphism to \\(H_c\\) and \\(e_s\\) arbitrary points of \\(H_c\\). Testing functions one étale component at a time shows that fullness is exactly fullness of the kernel tuple, since translation preserves the norm identity. Thus each component is \\(\\mathrm{Full}(H_c)\\times H_c^{e_s}\\), over all test algebras.</p><p>Finite freeness follows from Proposition 2 and the finite locally free kernel. Finite-level functoriality gives the action assertions. Local-cohomology transitions are injective by the regular-sequence colon identity. Rectangular ideals and powers of \\(\\mathfrak m'\\) are cofinal. Lemma 7.1 gives the tensor description, and a fixed finite \\(P_n\\)-resolution commutes with its finite-dimensional stages.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.3", "orig": "Scalar conjugation and relative translation cohomology", "depth": 1, "snippet": "On the cohomology rows of Lemma 7.2, a subgroup \\(\\mathcal T_h'=[p]^h\\mathcal T'\\) acts trivially.", "statement": "<p>On the cohomology rows of Lemma 7.2, a subgroup\n\\(\\mathcal T_h'=[p]^h\\mathcal T'\\) acts trivially. At an Artin thickness, its transported distribution algebra is\n\\[\n\\Lambda_N=R_N[[D_1,\\ldots,D_\\delta]],\\qquad\n\\delta=e_s(s-1),\\qquad I=(D_1,\\ldots,D_\\delta).\n\\]\nFor a row \\(V\\) annihilated by \\(I\\),\n\\[\nH^j(\\mathcal T_h',V)\n\\simeq V\\otimes_{R_N}\\bigwedge^j(I/I^2)^\\vee,\\qquad0\\leq j\\leq\\delta.\n\\]\nThe description is natural for distribution coordinates and commuting actions.</p>", "proof": "<p>The dual smaller group is formally smooth of dimension \\(s-1\\), so its distribution algebra is the displayed power-series ring. Finite dual-kernel quotients and augmentation powers are cofinal modulo the parameter ideal; nilpotence lifts this at every fixed Artin thickness.</p><p>By hypothesis choose a nonidentity scalar \\(a\\in1+p\\mathbb Z_p\\) acting identically on \\(H^*(P_n,S_s)\\), and take a power fixing the finite data in \\(M\\). It conjugates translations by \\([a]\\) or \\([a^{-1}]\\). The ideal\n\\[\nJ_a=(\\sigma_a(f)-f)\n\\]\nannihilates the row. Formal-group identities give\n\\[\nJ_a=([a-1]_1(D),\\ldots,[a-1]_\\delta(D)).\n\\]\nWriting \\(a-1=p^hu\\) identifies this with \\([p^h]^*I\\). Thus \\(\\mathcal T_h'\\) acts trivially, uniformly on the local-cohomology union. The inverse convention has the same valuation.</p><p>The variables form a regular sequence even over nilpotent \\(R_N\\). Their Koszul resolution gives zero differentials after Hom into \\(V\\), proving the exterior formula without flatness of \\(V\\). This is the relative torsor-descent Ext: completed bars and Koszul resolutions have continuous base-split contractions and comparison homotopies. The canonical augmentation conormal gives the nonlinear-coordinate naturality.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.4", "orig": "Individual matrix-group rows", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(V\\) be torsion-free pro-\\(p\\), compact \\(p\\)-adic analytic, and \\(C^\\bullet\\) a bounded complex of smooth \\(k[V]\\)-modules.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(V\\) be torsion-free pro-\\(p\\), compact \\(p\\)-adic analytic, and \\(C^\\bullet\\) a bounded complex of smooth \\(k[V]\\)-modules. If \\(R\\Gamma(V,C^\\bullet)\\) has finite-dimensional cohomology in each degree, then every\n\\[\nH^a(V,H^i(C^\\bullet))\n\\]\nis finite-dimensional. These vanish outside finitely many pairs \\((a,i)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Take full \\(k\\)-linear duals with product topology and put \\(\\Omega=k[[V]]\\). The pseudocompact complex \\(D^\\bullet=(C^\\bullet)^\\vee\\) has degreewise finite derived reduction\n\\[\nk\\widehat\\otimes_\\Omega^{\\,L}D^\\bullet\n\\simeq R\\Gamma(V,C^\\bullet)^\\vee.\n\\]\nAt the highest cohomology degree, right exactness identifies its reduction with the completed reduction of \\(H^b(D^\\bullet)\\). Lift finitely many generators to cycles. Their finite-free span is closed, and compact Nakayama kills the quotient with zero reduction. Thus \\(H^b(D^\\bullet)\\) is finitely generated.</p><p>Map those finite-free cycles into \\(D^\\bullet\\), take the cone, and repeat downward. Noetherianity controls the kernels inside finite-free modules, proving finite generation of every cohomology module. Such modules have finite-rank free pseudocompact resolutions, hence finite-dimensional \\(\\operatorname{Tor}^\\Omega_a(k,-)\\). The projective dimension of \\(k\\) is \\(\\dim V\\). Duality proves the assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "[Full frames and perfected norm-character rows; original Propositions 8.6 and 8.10.] Fix \\(1\\leq t<n<p-1\\), put \\(m=n-t\\), \\(G=GL_m(\\mathbb Z_p)\\), and assume the compact and bounded-pole conclusions of Propositions 6 …", "statement": "<p>[Full frames and perfected norm-character rows; original Propositions 8.6 and 8.10.]</p><p>Fix \\(1\\leq t&lt;n&lt;p-1\\), put \\(m=n-t\\), \\(G=GL_m(\\mathbb Z_p)\\), and assume the compact and bounded-pole conclusions of Propositions 6 and 7 at this pair. Define algebraic unions\n\\[\nB_{\\mathrm{conn},V}=\\operatorname*{colim}_{K\\subseteq P_t}B_{K,V},\n\\qquad\nB_{\\mathrm{full}}=\\operatorname*{colim}_{K,V}B_{K,V}.\n\\]</p><p>Then \\(H^*(P_n,B_{\\mathrm{conn},V})\\) has finite total dimension for every compact open \\(V\\subseteq G\\). One scalar subgroup \\(1+p^e\\mathbb Z_p\\subseteq G\\) acts identically on \\(H^*(P_n,B_{\\mathrm{full}})\\), also after algebraic perfection.</p><p>Moreover, for all \\(a,i,V\\),\n\\[\nH^a(V,H^i(P_n,B_{\\mathrm{full}}^{\\mathrm{perf}}))\n\\]\nis finite-dimensional and has a finite \\(P_t\\)-stable filtration with one-dimensional quotients powers of \\(\\nu_t\\). Its dual has the inverse-character filtration.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 8.1–8.6 prove full-connected finiteness and uniform scalar identity. Lemmas 8.7–8.10 follow actual root inclusions, remove lower translation degrees, and determine the perfected individual-row characters. These are precisely the two conclusions of the proposition.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "Lemma 8.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For a sufficiently deep \\(U\\) and transfer-stable compact \\(P\\), the comparison \\( H^i(P_n,P)\\to H^i(P_n,B_U) \\) identifies stable transfer images.", "statement": "<p>For a sufficiently deep \\(U\\) and transfer-stable compact \\(P\\), the comparison\n\\[\nH^i(P_n,P)\\to H^i(P_n,B_U)\n\\]\nidentifies stable transfer images. The dual transfer-colimit comparison of Proposition 5 therefore remains valid for the entire bounded-pole coefficient, preserving constant cochains and finite-level trace.</p>", "proof": "<p>Both groups are finite by the assumed compact and bounded-pole results. The comparison kernel is locally transfer-nilpotent. A basis of the target has representatives at a common pole bound, whose high transfer lies in \\(P\\). On finite vector spaces, stable transfer images are the maximal invertible parts; the two observations give an isomorphism on these parts.</p><p>On complexes take the natural zigzag\n\\[\nC^\\bullet(P_n,B_U)^*\n\\to C^\\bullet(P_n,P)^*\n\\leftarrow C^\\bullet(P_n,P)^\\vee\n\\]\nand transpose-transfer colimits. Compact images are closed, continuous and algebraic dualities are exact, and finite compact cohomology identifies their cohomology. The stable-image argument gives the other quasi-isomorphism. All maps are restriction, inclusion of functionals, or transpose transfer, so preserve the stated actions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.2", "orig": "Lemma 8.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For finite translation representations \\(N\\), write \\(C_U^\\bullet(N)=C^\\bullet(P_n,E_U(N))\\) using a fixed finite-projective \\(P_n\\)-resolution.", "statement": "<p>For finite translation representations \\(N\\), write\n\\(C_U^\\bullet(N)=C^\\bullet(P_n,E_U(N))\\) using a fixed finite-projective \\(P_n\\)-resolution. Form\n\\[\n\\mathcal D=\\operatorname*{colim}_{U,r}C_U^\\bullet(N_r)^*,\n\\]\nwith arrows dual to Hopf transfer and frame trace. It is a bounded rational translation complex with smooth commuting \\(U_0\\)-action and transpose constant-cochain action.</p><p>For \\(\\mathcal T_h=[p]^h\\mathcal T\\),\n\\[\nR\\Gamma(U,R\\Gamma(\\mathcal T_h,\\mathcal D))\n\\simeq C^\\bullet(P_n,B_{K,V}^{(h)})^*,\n\\qquad U=K\\times V.\n\\]\nHere \\(B_{K,V}^{(h)}\\) is the finite lift/root coefficient, with \\(h=0\\) the trivial translation representation. Frame corestriction is dual to coefficient pullback. Power transport preserves ordinary \\(\\mathbb F_p\\)-cochains.</p>", "proof": "<p>At a normal finite frame cover, faithful flat trivialization makes the coefficient a regular module for the finite deck group. Its projectivity descends, and trace identifies coinvariants with the lower coefficient. Finite sums and projective retracts in the \\(P_n\\)-resolution retain these facts. Duals are invariant-acyclic. The smooth union is computed by finite quotients because compact-source cochains into discrete smooth modules have finite image.</p><p>For translations, equivariant Hom from a finite rational \\(W\\) into a dual term is dual to the exact associated-bundle functor on \\(W^*\\). This proves injectivity: extend maps one finite subcomodule at a time. Restriction to \\(\\mathcal T_h\\) remains injective since induction across the finite quotient is exact. Invariants recover the required finite lift coefficient. The bounded complex permits translation calculation before compact-frame calculation.</p><p>At finite deck level, dual pullback is trace, and the induced invariant map is corestriction by the regular norm formula. Proposition 3 supplies power compatibility.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.3", "orig": "Proposition 8.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "After forgetting translations, \\( \\mathcal D\\otimes_kR \\simeq R\\Gamma_c(Z,\\mathscr A)\\otimes\\langle\\eta\\rangle[m+n^2]. \\) This is \\(U_0\\)-equivariant, linear for ordinary constant stabilizer cochains, and compatible …", "statement": "<p>After forgetting translations,\n\\[\n\\mathcal D\\otimes_kR\n\\simeq R\\Gamma_c(Z,\\mathscr A)\\otimes\\langle\\eta\\rangle[m+n^2].\n\\]\nThis is \\(U_0\\)-equivariant, linear for ordinary constant stabilizer cochains, and compatible with finite-frame trace.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Proposition 5 at fixed deep frame level and Lemma 8.1 to replace compact lattices by bounded-pole coefficients. Passing through all frame covers using transpose trace removes compact invariants: each finite locally constant deck-parameter diagram occurs at one finite quotient. Buffered support maps are defined on these diagrams before taking the union. Group duality, residue duality, and the common torsor nerve retain the actual structural constant action and the indicated shifts.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.4", "orig": "Lemma 8.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(M\\) be a rational translation representation with compatible scalar \\(a\\in1+p\\mathbb Z_p\\), \\(a\\neq1\\), conjugating translations by \\([a]\\) or \\([a^{-1}]\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(M\\) be a rational translation representation with compatible scalar \\(a\\in1+p\\mathbb Z_p\\), \\(a\\neq1\\), conjugating translations by \\([a]\\) or \\([a^{-1}]\\). If \\(a\\) acts identically on \\(M\\), then \\(\\mathcal T_h\\) acts trivially for sufficiently large \\(h\\). The same \\(h\\) works for any family with this same scalar identity.</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(t&gt;1\\), the ideal generated by \\(f\\circ[a]-f\\) annihilates \\(M\\). Formal-group subtraction shows that it contains the coordinates of \\([a-1]\\). Write \\(a-1=p^hu\\); the image of the augmentation ideal for \\(\\mathcal T_h\\) annihilates \\(M\\).</p><p>For \\(t=1\\), scalar conjugation carries \\(\\chi\\) to \\(a\\chi\\). Identity of the scalar forces \\((a-1)\\chi=0\\) on every occurring character, so \\(\\chi\\) is trivial on \\(\\mathcal T_h\\) for \\(h\\geq v_p(a-1)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.5", "orig": "Lemma 8.5", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For a finite-dimensional smooth \\(P_t\\)-representation \\(M\\), inverse limit over sufficiently small \\(K\\subseteq P_t\\), with corestriction, satisfies …", "statement": "<p>For a finite-dimensional smooth \\(P_t\\)-representation \\(M\\), inverse limit over sufficiently small \\(K\\subseteq P_t\\), with corestriction, satisfies\n\\[\n\\varprojlim_KH^c(K,M)=0\\quad(c\\neq t^2),\\qquad\n\\varprojlim_KH^{t^2}(K,M)\\simeq M.\n\\]\nFor a bounded smooth \\(P_t\\)-complex \\(C^\\bullet\\) with finite-dimensional cohomology,\n\\[\n\\varprojlim_KH^\\ell(R\\Gamma(K,C^\\bullet))\n\\simeq H^{\\ell-t^2}(C^\\bullet).\n\\]\nThese identifications are natural for degree-shifted maps and retain commuting actions and \\(P_t\\)-conjugation on normal levels.</p>", "proof": "<p>Shrink \\(K\\) to act trivially. Orientable group duality identifies dual corestriction with restriction in complementary degree. Positive-degree finite discrete cohomology vanishes in the restriction colimit, since every finite cocycle diagram factors through a quotient and becomes zero on its kernel; degree zero retains \\(M^\\vee\\).</p><p>For complexes, use the bounded group/cohomology spectral sequence. All groups at fixed levels are finite over finite \\(k\\), so inverse systems are Mittag–Leffler and inverse limit is exact. Only degree \\(t^2\\) survives. Naturality gives shifted-map compatibility. The adjoint determinant is one, so conjugation preserves the orientation.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.6", "orig": "Proposition 8.6", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The finite full-connected cohomology and uniform scalar assertions in Proposition 8 hold.", "statement": "<p>The finite full-connected cohomology and uniform scalar assertions in Proposition 8 hold.</p>", "proof": "<p>By Proposition 5, scalars in \\(1+p\\mathbb Z_p\\) act identically on supported cohomology and on the volume line. Lemma 8.3 and faithful almost extension give the same on \\(H^*(\\mathcal D)\\). Choose a nonidentity such scalar; Lemma 8.4 makes one \\(\\mathcal T_h\\) trivial on every cohomology row.</p><p>For \\(t&gt;1\\), the translation rows are\n\\[\nH^q(\\mathcal D)\\otimes_k\n\\bigwedge^b(\\mathfrak m_{\\Lambda_h}/\\mathfrak m_{\\Lambda_h}^2)^*,\n\\qquad0\\leq b\\leq d.\n\\]\nFor \\(t=1\\), only \\(b=0\\) occurs. Apply \\(V\\)-cohomology. After almost extension, finite flag resolutions and Shapiro reduce to compact parabolics with finite coefficient factors. Bounded finite-projective resolutions and the almost-length argument of Proposition 5 give finite-dimensional \\(k\\)-cohomology before extension.</p><p>Apply \\(K\\)-cohomology and inverse corestriction. Lemma 8.5 retains only degree \\(t^2\\), with the underlying finite coefficients. All spectral-sequence ranges are bounded. Lemma 8.2 identifies the result with the dual of\n\\(\\operatorname*{colim}_KH^*(P_n,B_{K,V}^{(h)})\\), which is therefore finite. Power transport gives \\(B_{\\mathrm{conn},V}\\).</p><p>The chosen scalar acts identically on the displayed row coefficients: it acts identically on \\(H^q(\\mathcal D)\\), and its cotangent action reduces to one. Its centrality makes conjugation on \\(V\\) trivial. The resulting cohomology filtrations have lengths bounded independently of frame levels. A fixed \\(p\\)-power kills their possible unipotent extension action, since \\((a^{p^j}-1)=(a-1)^{p^j}\\). Dualize and pass to the full-frame union. Smoothness extends identity from a generator to its open closed scalar subgroup. Power transport gives the same subgroup at every root stage.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.7", "orig": "Lemma 8.7", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In Lemma 8.2, the transpose of the actual root inclusion \\( B_{K,V}^{(h)}\\hookrightarrow B_{K,V}^{(h')} \\) is finite-Hopf corestriction …", "statement": "<p>In Lemma 8.2, the transpose of the actual root inclusion\n\\[\nB_{K,V}^{(h)}\\hookrightarrow B_{K,V}^{(h')}\n\\]\nis finite-Hopf corestriction\n\\[\nR\\Gamma(\\mathcal T_{h'},\\mathcal D)\\longrightarrow\nR\\Gamma(\\mathcal T_h,\\mathcal D).\n\\]\nIt commutes with frame corestrictions and connected-frame conjugation. If \\(t&gt;1\\) and \\(\\mathcal T_h\\) acts trivially on \\(H^j(\\mathcal D)\\), the row transition is zero in translation degrees \\(b&lt;d\\) and an oriented identity, up to nonzero scalar, in degree \\(d\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>In the normalized distribution model,\n\\[\nN_q=k[D_1,\\ldots,D_d]/(D_1^q,\\ldots,D_d^q),\n\\]\ninclusion of functions is multiplication by\n\\(\\prod_iD_i^{q'-q}\\), up to a unit; transfer is the opposite quotient. This is the actual root inclusion by Proposition 3.</p><p>Write \\(\\Lambda_h=k[[x_1,\\ldots,x_d]]\\) and\n\\(\\Lambda_{h'}=k[[y_1,\\ldots,y_d]]\\), with \\(y_i\\mapsto x_i^q\\). The socle inclusion is lifted on Koszul bases by\n\\[\ne_I\\longmapsto\\left(\\prod_{j\\notin I}x_j^{q-1}\\right)f_I.\n\\]\nApplying Hom into a row annihilated by all \\(x_i\\) gives zero below degree \\(d\\), and identity in degree \\(d\\). A unit changes the top map by its nonzero augmentation. The finite Hopf maps themselves are natural for nonlinear frame automorphisms, so the calculation retains their actions. At \\(t=1\\), the transpose is character projection and preserves the trivial character.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.8", "orig": "Lemma 8.8", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Use starting-height notation \\(s\\) in place of \\(t\\), and put \\(d=(n-s)(s-1)\\), \\(G=GL_{n-s}(\\mathbb Z_p)\\).", "statement": "<p>Use starting-height notation \\(s\\) in place of \\(t\\), and put \\(d=(n-s)(s-1)\\), \\(G=GL_{n-s}(\\mathbb Z_p)\\). Choose \\(h_0\\) with \\(\\mathcal T_{h_0}\\) trivial on \\(H^*(\\mathcal D)\\), and put\n\\[\nA_h=R\\Gamma(V,R\\Gamma(\\mathcal T_h,\\mathcal D)).\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hom}_k(H^i(P_n,B_{\\mathrm{conn},V}^{\\mathrm{perf}}),k)\n\\simeq\\varprojlim_{h,\\mathrm{cor}}H^{-i-s^2}(A_h)\n\\]\nas finite-dimensional \\(P_s\\)-representations.</p><p>Write \\(E_h^b=\\bigwedge^b(\\mathfrak m_{\\Lambda_h}/\\mathfrak m_{\\Lambda_h}^2)^*\\). If a character \\(\\chi\\) of \\(\\Delta_s=\\mu_{p^s-1}\\) satisfies\n\\[\nH^a(V,H^q(\\mathcal D)\\otimes E_h^d)_\\chi=0\n\\]\nfor all \\(a,q,h\\geq h_0\\), then the \\(\\chi\\)-part of this dual is zero. For \\(s=1\\), use \\(d=0\\), \\(E_h^0=k\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Finite flag and translation-row calculations in Lemma 8.6 give finite total \\(H^*(A_h)\\). Apply Lemma 8.5 at fixed \\(h\\), on normal connected levels, to obtain the corresponding finite-root dual. The adjoint determinant is one, so no additional \\(P_s\\)-character appears.</p><p>Power transport gives a uniform bound on finite-root dimensions, not an identification of root inclusions with isomorphisms. Thus their direct limit is finite-dimensional and dualizes to the stated inverse limit.</p><p>On each cohomology row, Lemma 8.7 kills all lower translation degrees under corestriction. The assumed \\(\\chi\\)-vanishing kills the top degree. Bounded \\(V\\)- and translation filtrations imply a uniformly bounded composite of root transitions is zero on that row’s \\(\\chi\\)-abutment. Applying the bounded cohomology filtration of \\(\\mathcal D\\) gives uniform pro-zero on \\(H^*(A_h)_\\chi\\). Thus its inverse limit vanishes. For \\(s=1\\), exact character projection gives the same conclusion directly. All limits are evaluated on finite \\(k\\)-systems before almost scalar extension.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.9", "orig": "Lemma 8.9", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every \\(i,V\\), the \\(P_s\\)-action on \\( H^i(P_n,B_{\\mathrm{conn},V}^{\\mathrm{perf}}) \\) has only powers of the norm character on \\(\\Delta_s\\).", "statement": "<p>For every \\(i,V\\), the \\(P_s\\)-action on\n\\[\nH^i(P_n,B_{\\mathrm{conn},V}^{\\mathrm{perf}})\n\\]\nhas only powers of the norm character on \\(\\Delta_s\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The orientation of an \\(r\\)-plane comes from its single constant-\\(\\mathbb F_p\\) compact-support line by the natural primitive comparison of Proposition 5. Its \\(P_s\\)-character takes values in \\(\\mathbb F_p^\\times\\), hence is a norm power. Rational plane and flag parameters are fixed by \\(P_s\\).</p><p>For a Teichmüller element \\(a\\in\\mathbb F_{p^s}^\\times\\), inverse substitution gives\n\\[\n\\langle\\eta\\rangle(a)=a^{-m},\\qquad\nE_{h_0}^d(a)=a^{-m(p+p^2+\\cdots+p^{s-1})},\n\\quad m=n-s.\n\\]\nFor the second formula, the covariant Honda Dieudonné module splits into rank-one eigensummands with residue characters\n\\(a,a^p,\\ldots,a^{p^{s-1}}\\). The Lie quotient has character \\(a\\), leaving cotangent characters \\(a^p,\\ldots,a^{p^{s-1}}\\) for the dual distribution group; top Ext is their inverse exterior product. Thus\n\\[\n(\\langle\\eta\\rangle\\otimes E_{h_0}^d)(a)=\\nu_s(a)^{-m}.\n\\]\nFor \\(s=1\\), the same formula holds with trivial Ext line.</p><p>Hence the top-translation support rows have only norm characters. Finite flag resolutions, \\(V\\)-cohomology, and faithful almost extension detect the vanishing condition of Lemma 8.8 for every nonnorm character. That lemma kills them in the perfected dual. Norm powers are closed under inversion, giving the primal assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.10", "orig": "Proposition 8.10", "depth": 1, "snippet": "At the current pair \\((n,s)\\), every \\( Q=H^a(V,H^i(P_n,B_{\\mathrm{full}}^{\\mathrm{perf}})) \\) is finite-dimensional and has a finite \\(P_s\\)-stable filtration by one-dimensional norm characters.", "statement": "<p>At the current pair \\((n,s)\\), every\n\\[\nQ=H^a(V,H^i(P_n,B_{\\mathrm{full}}^{\\mathrm{perf}}))\n\\]\nis finite-dimensional and has a finite \\(P_s\\)-stable filtration by one-dimensional norm characters.</p>", "proof": "<p>Take the algebraic matrix-level union in Lemma 8.9. The fixed \\(P_n\\)-resolution commutes with it, so \\(\\Delta_s^0=\\ker(\\nu_s|_{\\Delta_s})\\) acts identically on each actual \\(P_n\\)-cohomology row. It then acts identically after \\(V\\)-cohomology, since \\(V\\) commutes with \\(P_s\\).</p><p>Individual-row finiteness follows from Lemma 7.4: étale descent gives finite total \\(V'\\)-cohomology from Lemma 8.6 and the uniform finite root-stage dimensions, for each open \\(V'\\).</p><p>The image \\(\\Pi\\) of \\(P_s^1=1+\\mathfrak p_{D_s}\\) in \\(GL_k(Q)\\) is a finite normal \\(p\\)-group. Its augmentation-ideal filtration is \\(P_s\\)-stable. Quotients have trivial \\(P_s^1\\)-action and split into characters of \\(\\mathbb F_{p^s}^\\times\\). Each is trivial on \\(\\Delta_s^0\\), hence a norm power. Refine by these lines. Duality reverses the filtration and inverts its characters.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "Completion of coefficient finiteness", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every \\(1\\leq t<n<p-1\\):", "statement": "<p>For every \\(1\\leq t&lt;n&lt;p-1\\):</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Every admissible compact coefficient on \\(A\\) or \\(C_1\\), including the stable lattices above, has finite total \\(P_n\\)-cohomology.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Every finite joint frame \\(B_U\\), also with a fixed invariant-line power, finite direct sums, or finite-dimensional trivial factors, has finite total bounded-pole \\(P_n\\)-cohomology.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Every \\(B_{\\mathrm{conn},V}\\) has finite total cohomology. A scalar subgroup independent of levels acts identically on full-frame cohomology, also after algebraic root union. Every perfected individual matrix-group row is finite and has a finite connected-frame filtration by reduced-norm powers.</p><p>All assertions use common finite stages on profinite cochain sources.</p>", "proof": "<p>Induct on the lexicographic measure \\((n,n-t)\\). At \\(t=n\\), compact coefficients lie on a zero-dimensional disc and have finite-coefficient analytic-group cohomology.</p><p>At a current pair, Proposition 6 uses only compact assertions on higher starting-height dead-label discs, hence earlier pairs. It proves (1). Proposition 7 then uses current compact finiteness, full-frame assertions at \\((n,s)\\), \\(s&gt;t\\), and compact assertions at \\((s,t)\\), \\(s&lt;n\\); all are earlier in the measure. It proves (2). Proposition 8 uses current (1) and (2), and the support comparison, to prove (3), including its perfected norm-character rows. Thus no current full-frame assertion is used in the current compact or pole-removal proof. This completes the simultaneous induction.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "[Constant cochains and stable transport; original Theorem 9.1 and Proposition 9.5.] Let \\(1\\leq a<p-1\\), and put …", "statement": "<p>[Constant cochains and stable transport; original Theorem 9.1 and Proposition 9.5.]</p><p>Let \\(1\\leq a&lt;p-1\\), and put\n\\[\nK_a=GL_a(\\mathbb Z_p),\\quad\nI_a=\\{g\\in K_a:g\\bmod p\\text{ upper triangular}\\},\\quad\nP_a=\\mathcal O_{D_a}^{\\times}.\n\\]\nTheir continuous integral cohomology is finite free, exterior on degrees \\(1,3,\\ldots,2a-1\\), and reduces correctly modulo every \\(p^r\\); actual restriction \\(K_a\\to I_a\\) is a cohomology isomorphism.</p><p>For \\(n&lt;p-1\\), the common determinant-lattice modification stack gives natural multiplicative equivalences on finite Postnikov \\(p\\)-primary constant coefficients, and then on continuous \\(p\\)-complete sphere cochains,\n\\[\nC_{\\mathrm{cts}}^*(P_n;E)\n\\simeq C^*(\\mathcal M_n;E)_{(1)}\n\\simeq C_{\\mathrm{cts}}^*(J_n;E)\n\\simeq C_{\\mathrm{cts}}^*(K_n;E),\n\\]\nwhere \\(J_n=\\{g\\in GL_n(\\mathbb Q_p):v_p\\det g=0\\}\\), and \\((1)\\) is the generalized arithmetic eigenvalue-one summand.</p><p>On the independent extension locus \\(Z=(N_t^{n-t})_{\\mathrm{ind}}\\), after fixing the connected quotient frame, corresponding classes act by actual upper-left block restriction from \\(K_n\\) to \\(K_{n-t}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 10.1 proves constant cohomology and integral reduction. Lemmas 10.2–10.4 construct the actual geometric comparison maps. Lemma 10.5 separates their arithmetic primary part, transports finite and stable constants, and identifies the extension-space action with block restriction.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.1", "orig": "Theorem 9.1 and Remark 9.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For \\(G=K_a,I_a,P_a\\), \\( \\sum_q\\operatorname{rank}_{\\mathbb Z_p}H^q(G,\\mathbb Z_p)z^q =\\prod_{i=1}^a(1+z^{2i-1}), \\) and these groups are finite free, zero for \\(q>a^2\\).", "statement": "<p>For \\(G=K_a,I_a,P_a\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_q\\operatorname{rank}_{\\mathbb Z_p}H^q(G,\\mathbb Z_p)z^q\n=\\prod_{i=1}^a(1+z^{2i-1}),\n\\]\nand these groups are finite free, zero for \\(q&gt;a^2\\). Integral and finite-field cohomology have exterior presentations in these degrees,\n\\[\nH^q(G,\\mathbb Z_p)/p^r\\simeq H^q(G,\\mathbb Z/p^r).\n\\]\nRestriction \\(K_a\\to I_a\\) is an isomorphism integrally and modulo \\(p^r\\). Coefficient-field automorphisms act trivially on \\(H^*(P_a,\\mathbb Z_p)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Dotto–Le Hung’s split and division calculations give the mod-\\(p\\) exterior dimensions and Iwahori restriction when \\(a&lt;p-1\\). At \\(a=1\\), use \\(\\mathbb Z_p^\\times=\\mu_{p-1}\\times(1+p\\mathbb Z_p)\\).</p><p>These groups have no \\(p\\)-torsion and have finite-projective completed-group-ring resolutions of length \\(a^2\\). Integral cohomology is finitely generated. Rational Lazard comparison and splitting the division algebra give the same exterior dimensions. If \\(r_q\\) is integral rank and \\(t_q\\) counts cyclic \\(p\\)-torsion summands, then\n\\[\n\\dim_{\\mathbb F_p}H^q(G,\\mathbb F_p)=r_q+t_q+t_{q+1}.\n\\]\nEquality with rational dimensions forces every \\(t_q=0\\). Coefficient exact sequences give reduction, and mod-\\(p\\) restriction plus Nakayama gives integral restriction.</p><p>Exterior algebra descent from the division residue field follows by choosing homogeneous indecomposables over \\(\\mathbb F_p\\), lifting their odd generators, and comparing dimensions. Integral lifts give the integral exterior algebra by torsionfreeness and Nakayama.</p><p>Field automorphisms become inner on the split division Lie algebra, hence act trivially rationally; torsionfreeness gives integral triviality.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.2", "orig": "Lemma 9.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Restriction \\(K_a\\subseteq J_a\\) induces \\( C_{\\mathrm{cts}}^*(J_a;E)\\xrightarrow{\\simeq} C_{\\mathrm{cts}}^*(K_a;E) \\) for every finite Postnikov \\(p\\)-primary constant spectrum \\(E\\), and cohomology isomorphisms for …", "statement": "<p>Restriction \\(K_a\\subseteq J_a\\) induces\n\\[\nC_{\\mathrm{cts}}^*(J_a;E)\\xrightarrow{\\simeq}\nC_{\\mathrm{cts}}^*(K_a;E)\n\\]\nfor every finite Postnikov \\(p\\)-primary constant spectrum \\(E\\), and cohomology isomorphisms for \\(\\mathbb Z_p,\\mathbb Z/p^r\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(a&gt;1\\), \\(J_a\\) acts type-preservingly without inversions on the reduced building, with one closed chamber as fundamental domain. Vertex stabilizers are conjugates of \\(K_a\\); face stabilizers lie between an Iwahori and a vertex stabilizer. Their Iwahori indices are prime to \\(p\\), being flag counts congruent to one modulo \\(p\\). Transfer and Lemma 10.1 show all face-to-Iwahori restrictions are isomorphisms.</p><p>The bounded cellular spectral sequence has rows the cochain complex of a simplex with those constant cohomology groups, hence only degree-zero cohomology. Its edge is actual vertex restriction. Coefficient filtrations and finite Postnikov induction extend from \\(\\mathbb F_p\\) to \\(E\\). Finite-coefficient inverse systems are Mittag–Leffler, giving \\(\\mathbb Z_p\\). For \\(a=1\\), the groups coincide.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.3", "orig": "Lemma 9.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Choose a finite unramified \\(K/\\mathbb Q_p\\) defining the Honda endomorphisms and an algebraically closed completion \\(C\\).", "statement": "<p>Choose a finite unramified \\(K/\\mathbb Q_p\\) defining the Honda endomorphisms and an algebraically closed completion \\(C\\). Put\n\\[\n\\Omega_C^{n-1}=\\mathbb P_C^{n-1}\\setminus\\bigcup_HH_C,\n\\]\nwhere \\(H\\) runs through rational \\(\\mathbb Q_p\\)-hyperplanes. There is a determinant-lattice modification stack\n\\[\n\\mathcal M_n\\simeq[\\mathbb P_C^{n-1}/P_n]\n\\simeq[\\Omega_C^{n-1}/J_n],\n\\]\ncompatible with arithmetic over \\(K\\). Arithmetic acts trivially on the continuous group-cochain sources.</p>", "proof": "<p>A length-one quotient \\(V_n'\\to i_*\\mathcal L\\) has kernel of rank \\(n\\), degree zero. A positive-degree proper subbundle would contradict stability of \\(V_n'\\), so the kernel is slope zero and corresponds to a rational local system.</p><p>Conversely, an upper modification of a trivial rank-\\(n\\) bundle has no negative slopes and total degree one. Its nonbasic case has a slope-zero quotient, equivalent to a rational functional annihilating the modification direction. Absence of such a functional is exactly the Drinfeld locus. Relative slope classification and the slope-zero equivalence give inverse family descriptions and frame torsors.</p><p>Fix the determinant lattice. Rational frame changes preserving it are \\(J_n\\); positive-frame changes with zero reduced-norm valuation are \\(P_n\\). The Honda isocrystal can be defined so arithmetic commutes with its endomorphisms. Arithmetic determinant valuation is a continuous homomorphism from a profinite group to \\(\\mathbb Z\\), hence zero, so the chosen determinant component is preserved.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "[Finite constant-section construction of original Section 9.4.] For a small \\(v\\)-stack \\(X\\), locally profinite \\(G\\) acting on \\(X\\), and finite Postnikov \\(p\\)-primary \\(E\\), there is a natural structural map …", "statement": "<p>[Finite constant-section construction of original Section 9.4.]</p><p>For a small \\(v\\)-stack \\(X\\), locally profinite \\(G\\) acting on \\(X\\), and finite Postnikov \\(p\\)-primary \\(E\\), there is a natural structural map\n\\[\n\\eta_{X,G,E}:C_{\\mathrm{cts}}^*(G;E)\\longrightarrow\nC_v^*([X/G];E).\n\\]\nIt is induced by constant sections on finite clopen pieces of the action nerve, and respects continuous group homomorphisms, equivariant maps, base change, coefficient pairings, and torsor classifying pullbacks. For \\(X=\\operatorname{Spd}C\\) and compact \\(G\\), it is an equivalence.</p>", "proof": "<p>For compact profinite \\(T\\), use\n\\[\nLC(T;E)=\\operatorname*{colim}_{T\\twoheadrightarrow T_i}\n\\prod_{t\\in T_i}E.\n\\]\nConstant sections on inverse-image clopen pieces give\n\\(LC(T;E)\\to C_v^*(X\\times T;E)\\). For locally profinite powers take products over disjoint compact-open decompositions and common refinements. Totalizing the nerve gives \\(\\eta\\). Every compatibility holds termwise, including cup pairings.</p><p>For an \\(H\\)-torsor and \\(H\\to G\\), the induced \\(G\\)-torsor’s structural map is the original map followed by restriction. This identifies the actual classifying pullbacks used later.</p><p>Finite constant étale and \\(v\\)-cochains agree on locally spatial parameter products. Over algebraically closed \\(C\\), the point is strictly totally disconnected, and continuity on inverse limits of finite disjoint copies of the point gives\n\\[\nLC(T;HM)\\simeq C_v^*(\\operatorname{Spd}C\\times T;HM).\n\\]\nFinite Postnikov induction and compact nerve totalization give the last assertion. The compact projective-resolution comparison is the one in Proposition 5, with the coefficient unit fixed before choosing the resolution.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.5", "orig": "Proposition 9.5", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Choose \\(\\gamma\\in\\operatorname{Gal}(\\overline K/K)\\) whose mod-\\(p\\) cyclotomic value \\(u\\) generates \\(\\mathbb F_p^\\times\\).", "statement": "<p>Choose \\(\\gamma\\in\\operatorname{Gal}(\\overline K/K)\\) whose mod-\\(p\\) cyclotomic value \\(u\\) generates \\(\\mathbb F_p^\\times\\). Actual structural pullbacks identify\n\\[\nH^*(P_n,\\mathbb F_p)\n\\simeq H^*(\\mathcal M_n,\\mathbb F_p)_{(1)}\n\\simeq H^*(J_n,\\mathbb F_p)\n\\]\nmultiplicatively. They extend to the finite Postnikov and sphere equivalences in Proposition 10.</p><p>For \\(m=n-t\\), let\n\\[\nb:[Z/K_m]\\to BP_n,\\qquad q:[Z/K_m]\\to BK_m\n\\]\nclassify the positive middle frame and the kernel frame, after fixing the connected quotient frame. Corresponding classes satisfy\n\\[\nb^*c_P=q^*\\operatorname{res}_{K_m}^{K_n}c_K,\n\\qquad g\\longmapsto\\operatorname{diag}(g,1_t).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Projective-space geometric cohomology is \\(\\mathbb F_p(-j)\\) in degree \\(2j\\), \\(0\\leq j&lt;n\\). Integral and finite-coefficient Drinfeld-space cohomology, by Colmez–Dospinescu–Nizioł, is the continuous dual generalized Steinberg representation twisted by \\(-j\\), in degree \\(j\\), \\(0\\leq j&lt;n\\). Arithmetic acts trivially on the representation factor, hence by \\(u^{-j}\\) on each geometric row.</p><p>Use finite compact resolutions, and the finite chamber for \\(J_n\\), in geometric descent. All higher geometric rows have eigenvalue different from one because \\(n-1&lt;p-1\\). Primary decomposition is exact, so the generalized-one part is exactly the geometric degree-zero row. Lemma 10.4 identifies its edge with the actual constant-section unit: \\(1\\) is nonzero and spans geometric \\(H^0\\). This proves the specified multiplicative maps.</p><p>For finite Postnikov coefficients, take\n\\[\nQ(T)=\\prod_{j=1}^{n-1}(T-\\widetilde{u^{-j}}),\\qquad Q(1)\\in\\mathbb Z_p^\\times.\n\\]\nBounded filtrations, coefficient extensions, and Postnikov induction give coprime primary factors \\((T-1)^N,Q(T)^N\\). On bounded \\(p\\)-primary spectra, increase powers so the corresponding polynomial maps vanish, then use Bézout idempotents modulo a sufficient \\(p\\)-power. This defines the exact functorial primary summand. The same finite units give equivalences and product and Hurewicz compatibility.</p><p>Define the sphere limit by\n\\[\nC_{\\mathrm{cts}}^*(G;S)=\n\\varprojlim_{r,N}C_{\\mathrm{cts}}^*(G;\\tau_{\\leq N}(S/p^r)),\n\\]\nand define the middle primary object by this same limit. No unrestricted eigenspace/limit interchange is made. Finite cohomological dimension and finite-coefficient Mittag–Leffler control identify the continuous model.</p><p>For block compatibility, choose a quotient \\(V_t\\to i_*\\mathcal L\\) and a determinant-matched rational frame of its slope-zero kernel \\(W_t\\). Modify the universal extension to obtain\n\\[\n0\\to W_n\\to V_n'\\to i_*\\mathcal L\\to0,\\qquad\n0\\to\\mathcal L_m\\otimes\\mathcal O\\to W_n\\to W_t\\to0.\n\\]\nThis gives a map to \\(\\mathcal M_n\\). Its slope-zero frame group is\n\\[\nH_{m,t}=\n\\left\\{\\begin{pmatrix}g&amp;v\\\\0&amp;1_t\\end{pmatrix}:\ng\\in K_m,\\ v\\in M_{m,t}(\\mathbb Q_p)\\right\\}\\subseteq J_n.\n\\]\nThe additive upper block has no positive finite constant cohomology. Exhaust it by \\(p^{-r}\\mathbb Z_p^{mt}\\); positive-degree restrictions are zero modulo \\(p\\), degree zero is identity, and cochain restrictions are surjective. Mittag–Leffler, coefficient, and Postnikov arguments prove the assertion, also with compact parameters. Inflation from \\(K_m\\) is therefore an equivalence, inverse to its diagonal section.</p><p>Restriction through that section is exactly the upper-left inclusion into \\(K_n\\). Torsor naturality of Lemma 10.4 pulls the equality of the two \\(\\mathcal M_n\\)-classes to the displayed equality on \\([Z/K_m]\\). It persists in the sphere limit and for cup actions on supports; no middle-frame map outside \\(Z\\) is required.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "Stable primitive generators", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(p\\) be odd and \\(1\\leq N\\leq p-2\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(p\\) be odd and \\(1\\leq N\\leq p-2\\). For \\(G_a=GL_a(\\mathbb Z_p)\\), there are classes\n\\[\nc_{a,i}\\in\\pi_{1-2i}C_{\\mathrm{cts}}^*(G_a;S),\n\\qquad1\\leq i\\leq a\\leq N,\n\\]\nwith leading integral classes\n\\(\\alpha_{a,i}\\in H^{2i-1}(G_a,\\mathbb Z_p)\\), such that:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Their integral and mod-\\(p\\) exterior algebras are the full constant cohomology algebras. Their rationalizations are nonzero multiples of the standard primitive classes; \\(\\alpha_{a,1}\\) is divided determinant logarithm.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. For upper-left \\(G_b\\subseteq G_a\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{res}\\alpha_{a,i}=\n\\begin{cases}\\alpha_{b,i},&amp;i\\leq b,\\\\0,&amp;i&gt;b,\\end{cases}\n\\]\nand sphere representatives satisfy \\(\\operatorname{res}c_{a,i}=c_{b,i}\\) for \\(i\\leq b\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Proposition 10 transports the rank-\\(N\\) representatives to\n\\(u_i\\in\\pi_{1-2i}C_{\\mathrm{cts}}^*(P_N;S)\\), with leading mod-\\(p\\) classes \\(\\xi_i\\). On an extension space with \\(m\\)-dimensional Tate kernel, their ordinary action is \\(\\overline\\alpha_{m,i}\\) for \\(i\\leq m\\), and zero for \\(i&gt;m\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 11.8–11.9 show that\n\\[\n\\alpha_{a,1}\\cdots\\alpha_{a,a}\n=\\left(\\prod_i\\epsilon_i\\right)p^{-a(a+1)/2}b_1\\cdots b_a\n\\]\nis an integral top generator, so its reduction is nonzero. The odd classes give an exterior-algebra map. A nonzero homogeneous kernel element could be multiplied, using the source’s perfect top pairing, to a nonzero top kernel class, impossible. Proposition 10’s dimensions give a mod-\\(p\\) isomorphism; torsionfreeness and Nakayama give the integral one.</p><p>The standard representation and fixed \\(f_i\\) give exact block compatibility of leading classes. Above the smaller rank, rational primitives vanish, hence integral restrictions vanish. Choose sphere representatives at rank \\(N\\) by Lemma 11.4 and restrict them to lower ranks. Proposition 10 transports the rank-\\(N\\) classes and their Hurewicz images, and identifies their actual action with block restriction.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.1", "orig": "Lemma 10.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The reduced standard-representation classes \\(\\kappa_{a,r}\\) for \\(GL_a(\\mathbb Z/p^r)\\) define a continuous additive class \\( \\kappa_a\\in\\pi_0C_{\\mathrm{cts}}^*(G_a;K(\\mathbb Z_p)_p^\\wedge). \\) Composition with a map …", "statement": "<p>The reduced standard-representation classes\n\\(\\kappa_{a,r}\\) for \\(GL_a(\\mathbb Z/p^r)\\) define a continuous additive class\n\\[\n\\kappa_a\\in\\pi_0C_{\\mathrm{cts}}^*(G_a;K(\\mathbb Z_p)_p^\\wedge).\n\\]\nComposition with a map to a complete connective finite-type spectrum is represented in the continuous Postnikov construction, respects blocks and block sums, and agrees on the discrete group with the usual \\(K\\)-theory class followed by completion.</p>", "proof": "<p>At a fixed coefficient exponent and bounded Postnikov interval, \\(p\\)-adic \\(K\\)-theory continuity identifies the constant system \\(K(\\mathbb Z_p)/p^l\\) with the pro-system \\(K(\\mathbb Z/p^r)/p^l\\) on homotopy groups. Finite-ring \\(K\\)-groups with finite coefficients are finite, giving Mittag–Leffler and pro-Postnikov equivalence on the interval.</p><p>Thus maps to the bounded targets are represented far enough down the finite-ring tower, through finite congruence quotients of \\(G_a\\). The pro equivalence retains compatible homotopies. Taking coefficient and Postnikov limits gives \\(\\kappa_a\\) and its composites. Finite-stage block additivity and discrete standard-representation naturality persist in the limits. Bounded ordinary components are available at arbitrary matrix ranks.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.2", "orig": "Lemma 10.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For \\(2\\leq i\\leq p-2\\), there are maps \\( f_i:K(\\mathbb Z_p)_p^\\wedge\\longrightarrow\\Sigma^{2i-1}\\ell_p, \\qquad \\pi_*\\ell_p=\\mathbb Z_p[v],\\ |v|=2p-2, \\) inducing generator coordinates on the free degree-\\((2i-1)\\) …", "statement": "<p>For \\(2\\leq i\\leq p-2\\), there are maps\n\\[\nf_i:K(\\mathbb Z_p)_p^\\wedge\\longrightarrow\\Sigma^{2i-1}\\ell_p,\n\\qquad \\pi_*\\ell_p=\\mathbb Z_p[v],\\ |v|=2p-2,\n\\]\ninducing generator coordinates on the free degree-\\((2i-1)\\) groups. The integral étale edge and localization identify these groups with\n\\(H^1(\\mathbb Q_p,\\mathbb Z_p(i))\\), up to a \\(p\\)-adic unit.</p>", "proof": "<p>The local \\(K\\)-theory/TC comparison and odd-prime TC eigensplitting give the nonexceptional summands \\(\\Sigma^{2i-1}\\ell_p\\); projection defines \\(f_i\\). For \\(d=2i-1\\) in this range, the decomposition gives\n\\[\n\\pi_dK(\\mathbb Z_p)_p^\\wedge=\\mathbb Z_p,\\qquad\n\\pi_{d-1}K(\\mathbb Z_p)_p^\\wedge=0.\n\\]\nThe coefficient sequence identifies the mod-\\(p^r\\) degree with this free group modulo \\(p^r\\). Localization has no positive residue contribution because\n\\(K(\\mathbb F_p)_p^\\wedge\\simeq H\\mathbb Z_p\\). Local-field étale descent gives the compatible unique odd-degree edge\n\\[\n\\pi_dK(\\mathbb Z_p)_p^\\wedge/p^r\n\\simeq H^1(\\mathbb Q_p,\\mu_{p^r}^{\\otimes i}).\n\\]\nFinite degree-zero groups remove the inverse-limit error, giving the integral identification.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.3", "orig": "Lemma 10.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\( z_{a,i}=f_i\\kappa_a\\in\\pi_{1-2i}C_{\\mathrm{cts}}^*(G_a;\\ell_p), \\) and let \\(\\alpha_{a,i}\\) be its leading integral class.", "statement": "<p>Let\n\\[\nz_{a,i}=f_i\\kappa_a\\in\\pi_{1-2i}C_{\\mathrm{cts}}^*(G_a;\\ell_p),\n\\]\nand let \\(\\alpha_{a,i}\\) be its leading integral class. Normalize \\(b_i\\) as the rational primitive transgressing the algebraic de Rham Chern class \\(c_i\\).</p><p>For \\(a\\leq p-2\\), \\(i\\leq a\\), the only possibly nonzero rational ordinary component of \\(z_{a,i}\\) is degree \\(2i-1\\), and \\(\\alpha_{a,i}\\otimes\\mathbb Q_p\\) is a scalar multiple of \\(b_i\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Block additivity makes every rational component primitive. Work on a bounded coefficient Postnikov interval, where Lemma 11.1 constructs components at all ranks; clear its finitely many denominators before rationalizing. Stable rational matrix Lie cohomology has one primitive \\(b_j\\) in degree \\(2j-1\\), vanishing on rank \\(a&lt;j\\).</p><p>Possible component degrees are\n\\[\n2i-1+2r(p-1)=2(i+r(p-1))-1.\n\\]\nFor \\(r&gt;0\\), the primitive index exceeds \\(a\\leq p-2\\), so it vanishes.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.4", "orig": "Lemma 10.5", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every \\(\\alpha_{a,i}\\), \\(1\\leq i\\leq a\\leq p-2\\), has a sphere-cochain representative.", "statement": "<p>Every \\(\\alpha_{a,i}\\), \\(1\\leq i\\leq a\\leq p-2\\), has a sphere-cochain representative. For \\(i\\geq2\\), its \\(\\ell_p\\)-image is \\(z_{a,i}\\). For \\(i=1\\), take determinant pullback of\n\\[\n\\lambda(u)=\\frac{\\log u}{\\log(1+p)}:\\mathbb Z_p^\\times\\to\\mathbb Z_p.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(q_0=2p-2\\). Factoring \\(\\psi^q-1\\) through the positive cover gives\n\\[\n\\theta:\\ell_p\\to\\Sigma^{q_0}\\ell_p,\\qquad j_p=\\operatorname{fib}\\theta,\n\\]\nwith sphere unit \\(S\\to j_p\\).</p><p>The continuous cochain spectral sequence has \\(s\\leq a^2\\). Its integral terms for suspensions of \\(\\ell_p\\) are free by Proposition 10. Rational differentials vanish because the relevant finite range splits into rational Eilenberg–Mac Lane spectra. Induction makes all integral differentials zero, and the abutment torsion-free. Lemma 11.3 says \\(\\theta z_{a,i}\\) is rationally zero; therefore it is zero, and a nullhomotopy lifts \\(z_{a,i}\\) to \\(j_p\\)-cochains.</p><p>The sphere-to-\\(j_p\\) map is an isomorphism on homotopy below the first beta stem\n\\[\n2p(p-1)-2.\n\\]\nSince \\(a^2\\leq(p-2)^2\\) is smaller, and coefficients above \\(a^2\\) cannot contribute to negative total degrees, the lift has a sphere representative.</p><p>For \\(i=1\\), \\(\\mathbb Z_p^\\times\\) has cohomological dimension one, so total degree \\(-1\\) has only its \\(H^1(\\mathbb Z_p^\\times,\\mathbb Z_p)\\) entry. The divided logarithm lifts and pulls back along determinant.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.5", "orig": "Lemma 10.6", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For \\(2\\leq i\\leq p-2\\), \\(d=2i-1\\), and \\(X=K(\\mathbb Z_p)_p^\\wedge\\), there is an integral isomorphism \\( c_i^S:\\pi_dX\\xrightarrow{\\simeq}H^1(\\mathbb Q_p,\\mathbb Z_p(i)) \\) whose value on a completed discrete …", "statement": "<p>For \\(2\\leq i\\leq p-2\\), \\(d=2i-1\\), and \\(X=K(\\mathbb Z_p)_p^\\wedge\\), there is an integral isomorphism\n\\[\nc_i^S:\\pi_dX\\xrightarrow{\\simeq}H^1(\\mathbb Q_p,\\mathbb Z_p(i))\n\\]\nwhose value on a completed discrete \\(K_d(\\mathbb Z_p)\\)-class rationalizes to the Soulé regulator used by Huber–Kings.</p>", "proof": "<p>Match Moore-space and spectrum conventions:\n\\[\n[P^d(q),\\Omega^\\infty_0Y]=\\pi_d(Y/q).\n\\]\nCompose Lemma 11.2’s finite-coefficient identifications with universal étale Chern maps \\(c_{i,1}\\). Their odd-coefficient additivity and coefficient compatibility give a continuous \\(\\mathbb Z_p\\)-linear inverse-limit map.</p><p>Modulo \\(p\\), pass to \\(L=\\mathbb Q_p(\\zeta_p)\\). The Chern-map cokernel is killed by \\((i-1)!\\), a unit, hence the map is onto over \\(L\\). Chern transfer compatibility holds after multiplication by \\(i\\), also a unit. Restriction followed by corestriction is multiplication by \\(p-1\\), so the map is onto over \\(\\mathbb Q_p\\). Both source and target are one-dimensional modulo \\(p\\), and integral rank one, making \\(c_i^S\\) an isomorphism.</p><p>For a discrete class, finite Chern evaluation is evaluation of the same universal integral class on its finite bar Hurewicz cycle. Compatibility at every coefficient level and inverse limit identify it with \\(c_i^S\\). Rational coefficient image is the Huber–Kings Soulé regulator, normalized by the same line-bundle Chern class and splitting principle. Compact-source rational cochains have bounded image, so the rational target is the localization of the integral target.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.6", "orig": "Lemma 10.7", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For \\(2\\leq i\\leq p-2\\), in the standard period basis, \\( \\exp_{\\mathrm{BK}}^{-1}H^1(\\mathbb Q_p,\\mathbb Z_p(i))=p^i\\mathbb Z_p. \\)", "statement": "<p>For \\(2\\leq i\\leq p-2\\), in the standard period basis,\n\\[\n\\exp_{\\mathrm{BK}}^{-1}H^1(\\mathbb Q_p,\\mathbb Z_p(i))=p^i\\mathbb Z_p.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The integral group is free of rank one: local cohomology is finitely generated, rational rank is one, and torsion vanishes because the mod-\\(p\\) cyclotomic \\(i\\)-th power is nontrivial.</p><p>Use covariant filtered Frobenius weights \\(-i,0\\); duality places them in Fontaine–Laffaille’s interval \\([0,i]\\subseteq[0,p-2]\\). Its exact lattice functor, finite full faithfulness, and rational comparison apply.</p><p>The endpoint lattice has standard period scale. In the period ring\n\\[\n\\mathcal S=W(\\mathcal O_{C^\\flat})[\\xi^p/p],\\qquad\n\\mathcal S^j=(\\xi^j,\\xi^p/p),\n\\]\nthe logarithm \\(t_{\\mathrm{cyc}}=\\log[\\epsilon]\\) lies in the completed \\(\\mathcal S^1\\), and \\(t_{\\mathrm{cyc}}^i\\) is fixed by divided Frobenius on the completed \\(\\mathcal S^i\\). The series estimate follows by writing powers as \\(k=pm+r\\), for which \\(m-v_p(k)\\to\\infty\\). The residue map \\(x\\mapsto\\theta(x/\\xi^i)\\) is integral, whereas\n\\[\nv_p\\theta((t_{\\mathrm{cyc}}^i/p)/\\xi^i)\n=\\frac{i}{p-1}-1&lt;0.\n\\]\nThus \\(t_{\\mathrm{cyc}}^i\\), but not its quotient by \\(p\\), belongs to the rank-one realization. Its lattice is exactly \\(\\mathbb Z_pt_{\\mathrm{cyc}}^i\\).</p><p>With standard covariant basis \\(e_i\\), write\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Fil}^0=\\mathbb Z_p(e_0+xe_i),\\qquad\n\\phi(e_i)=p^{-i}e_i,\\qquad\\phi(e_0)=e_0+ye_i.\n\\]\nStrong divisibility is\n\\[\nx\\in\\mathbb Z_p,\\qquad z=y+p^{-i}x\\in\\mathbb Z_p.\n\\]\nSubtracting the rational Frobenius splitting gives exponential parameter\n\\[\nx-\\frac{y}{1-p^{-i}}=\\frac{x-z}{1-p^{-i}},\n\\]\nwhose range is \\(p^i\\mathbb Z_p\\).</p><p>For \\(x=1,z=0\\), the reduced filtered extension does not split: on the dual, the required filtered lift is \\(f_i-f_0\\), but divided Frobenius sends it to \\(f_i\\). Finite full faithfulness makes the Galois extension nonsplit modulo \\(p\\), hence primitive. Its parameter \\(p^i/(p^i-1)\\) generates the full integral lattice.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.7", "orig": "Lemma 10.8", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For \\(2\\leq i\\leq p-2\\), the discrete Soulé regulator \\( K_{2i-1}(\\mathbb Z_p)\\otimes_{\\mathbb Z}\\mathbb Q_p \\longrightarrow H^1(\\mathbb Q_p,\\mathbb Q_p(i)) \\) is nonzero.", "statement": "<p>For \\(2\\leq i\\leq p-2\\), the discrete Soulé regulator\n\\[\nK_{2i-1}(\\mathbb Z_p)\\otimes_{\\mathbb Z}\\mathbb Q_p\n\\longrightarrow H^1(\\mathbb Q_p,\\mathbb Q_p(i))\n\\]\nis nonzero.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a nonidentity Teichmüller root \\(\\xi\\), and use its weight-\\(i\\) polylogarithmic symbol over \\(\\mathbb Q(\\xi)\\). Its differential vanishes because \\(\\xi\\) is torsion. The number-field polylogarithmic-to-\\(K\\)-theory map, localization at the chosen \\(p\\)-adic prime, and the normalized syntomic regulator give value\n\\(\\pm(i-1)!\\operatorname{Li}_i(\\xi)\\); logarithmic corrections vanish.</p><p>Coleman’s measure identity gives\n\\[\n(1-p^{-i})\\operatorname{Li}_i(\\xi)\n=\\lim_r\n\\frac{\\sum_{1\\leq b&lt;p^r,\\ p\\nmid b}\\xi^bb^{-i}}\n{1-\\xi^{p^r}}.\n\\]\nModulo \\(p\\), its numerator is\n\\[\nP_i(X)=\\sum_{b=1}^{p-1}b^{-i}X^b.\n\\]\nThis nonzero degree-\\((p-1)\\) polynomial vanishes at \\(0,1\\), but cannot vanish on all \\(\\mathbb F_p\\). Some nonidentity \\(\\xi\\) therefore gives nonzero value. Compatibility of normalized syntomic Chern character, usual Chern classes, and the Huber–Kings exponential comparison yields nonzero étale Soulé regulator. Localization supplies a preimage in \\(K_{2i-1}(\\mathbb Z_p)\\otimes\\mathbb Q_p\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.8", "orig": "Lemma 10.9", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For fixed \\(f_i\\), there are units \\(\\epsilon_i\\in\\mathbb Z_p^\\times\\), independent of rank, with \\( \\alpha_{a,i}\\otimes\\mathbb Q_p=\\epsilon_ip^{-i}b_i, \\qquad1\\leq i\\leq a\\leq p-2. \\) For \\(i=1\\), use the divided …", "statement": "<p>For fixed \\(f_i\\), there are units \\(\\epsilon_i\\in\\mathbb Z_p^\\times\\), independent of rank, with\n\\[\n\\alpha_{a,i}\\otimes\\mathbb Q_p=\\epsilon_ip^{-i}b_i,\n\\qquad1\\leq i\\leq a\\leq p-2.\n\\]\nFor \\(i=1\\), use the divided logarithm.</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(i\\geq2\\), let \\(g_i=(f_i)_*\\), and set\n\\[\nt_i=\\exp_{\\mathrm{BK}}^{-1}\n\\bigl(c_i^S(g_i^{-1}(1))\\bigr)\\in p^i\\mathbb Z_p^\\times\n\\]\nby Lemmas 11.5–11.6.</p><p>Discrete Hurewicz evaluation of the bottom Postnikov composite defining \\(\\alpha_i\\) gives\n\\[\n\\langle\\alpha_i^\\delta,\\operatorname{hur}(x)\\rangle=g_i(\\widehat x).\n\\]\nHuber–Kings and Lemma 11.5 give\n\\[\n\\langle b_i^\\delta,\\operatorname{hur}(x)\\rangle\n=\\exp_{\\mathrm{BK}}^{-1}r_p(x)\n=t_i g_i(\\widehat x).\n\\]\nChoose \\(x\\) detected by Lemma 11.7. Lemma 11.3’s primitive scalar is therefore \\(t_i^{-1}=\\epsilon_ip^{-i}\\). All coordinates were rank independent. For \\(i=1\\), \\(v_p\\log(1+p)=1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.9", "orig": "Lemma 10.10", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(a\\leq p-2\\), \\(d=a^2\\), and order the matrix entries.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(a\\leq p-2\\), \\(d=a^2\\), and order the matrix entries. Put\n\\[\n\\omega_a=\\bigwedge_{r,s}(g^{-1}dg)_{rs}\n=\\det(g)^{-a}\\bigwedge_{r,s}dg_{rs}.\n\\]\nThen:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. \\(b_1\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge b_a=u_a\\omega_a\\), with \\(u_a\\in\\mathbb Z_{(p)}^\\times\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. For sufficiently large \\(r\\), the integral top lattice of\n\\(U_r=1+p^rM_a(\\mathbb Z_p)\\) corresponds under rational Lazard comparison to\n\\(\\mathbb Z_pp^{-rd}\\omega_a\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. The top lattice of \\(G_a\\) corresponds to\n\\(\\mathbb Z_pp^{-a(a+1)/2}\\omega_a\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The suspended-Chern comparison and volume calculation on \\(U(a)\\) give\n\\[\nu_a=\\pm\\prod_{j=1}^a(j-1)!,\n\\]\na \\(p\\)-unit. Successive last-column projections to \\(S^{2j-1}\\) yield\n\\[\n\\int_{U(a)}\\omega_a\n=\\pm\\frac{(2\\pi\\sqrt{-1})^{a(a+1)/2}}\n{\\prod_{j=1}^a(j-1)!}.\n\\]</p><p>For the integral assertion let \\(e=v_p(d!)\\) and \\(r\\geq d+1\\). In coordinates \\(g=1+p^rY\\), integrate\n\\[\np^{-rd}\\omega_a=\\det(1+p^rY)^{-a}\\bigwedge dY_{rs}\n\\]\nformally over affine simplices. Monomial denominators divide \\((h+d)!\\), while positive-degree coefficients have factors \\(p^{rh}\\), giving convergence. Affine left multiplication and formal Stokes make the result a continuous cocycle \\(C_r\\), whose leading term is \\(\\det(X_1,\\ldots,X_d)/d!\\). Differentiation cancels this \\(d!\\), giving the stated form.</p><p>Modulo \\(p^{e+1}\\), every positive-degree term of \\(d!C_r\\) vanishes since\n\\(e+rh-v_p((h+d)!)\\geq e+1\\). Product vertices reduce to additive ones. Entry functions\n\\[\n\\chi_j(g)=((g-1)/p^r)_j\\bmod p^{e+1}\n\\]\nare homomorphisms, and\n\\[\n[d!C_r]=d![\\chi_1\\smile\\cdots\\smile\\chi_d].\n\\]\nTheir mod-\\(p\\) cup is the uniform-group top generator. Oriented duality gives top integral cohomology \\(\\mathbb Z_p\\) and no next degree. Thus \\(d!C_r\\) has exactly valuation \\(e\\), and \\(C_r\\) is an integral unit generator. Division occurs only after this valuation determination.</p><p>Top restriction from \\(G_a\\) to \\(U_r\\) multiplies an orientation generator by the index, up to a unit. Its valuation is\n\\[\nv_p[G_a:U_r]=ra^2-\\frac{a(a+1)}2.\n\\]\nCombining with the \\(U_r\\)-lattice proves (3).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "Actual constant-cochain module", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Fix \\(1\\leq t<n<p-1\\), put \\(m=n-t\\), \\(G=GL_m(\\mathbb Z_p)\\), and \\( B_{n,t}=B_{\\mathrm{conn},G}. \\) Let \\(\\xi_i\\in H^{2i-1}(P_n,k)\\) be the transported reductions of the fixed rank-\\(n\\) classes of Proposition 11.", "statement": "<p>Fix \\(1\\leq t&lt;n&lt;p-1\\), put \\(m=n-t\\), \\(G=GL_m(\\mathbb Z_p)\\), and\n\\[\nB_{n,t}=B_{\\mathrm{conn},G}.\n\\]\nLet \\(\\xi_i\\in H^{2i-1}(P_n,k)\\) be the transported reductions of the fixed rank-\\(n\\) classes of Proposition 11. Then\n\\[\n\\bigwedge_k(e_1,\\ldots,e_m)\n\\xrightarrow{\\simeq}H^*(P_n,B_{n,t}),\n\\qquad e_i\\longmapsto\\xi_i\\cdot1,\\quad |e_i|=2i-1.\n\\]\nThus \\(\\xi_I\\cdot1\\), \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}\\), form the specified basis, in cohomological degree \\(d(I)\\) and internal degree zero. Classes \\(\\xi_i\\) for \\(i&gt;m\\) act as zero. The same rank-\\(n\\) family works for every \\(t\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>By Propositions 10–11,\n\\[\nA_G=\\bigwedge_k(\\overline\\alpha_{m,1},\\ldots,\\overline\\alpha_{m,m}),\n\\qquad\\sum_{i=1}^m(2i-1)=m^2.\n\\]\nLemma 12.3 identifies the dual desired module with the reindexed regular module for these specified generators. The exterior algebra’s perfect top pairing identifies its reindexed dual with its regular module, generated in degree zero.</p><p>Proposition 9 gives finiteness. The comparison makes \\(H^0(P_n,B_{\\mathrm{conn},G})\\) one-dimensional. Its unit is nonzero: the algebraic coefficient rings are unital and inject \\(k\\cdot1\\) into their union, and degree zero has no negative-cochain boundary. Thus \\(1\\) is the degree-zero generator.</p><p>The stated cup map is therefore an isomorphism after faithful almost extension, hence before it. Odd squares vanish because \\(p\\) is odd, so it is an algebra isomorphism. Higher generators restrict to zero by Proposition 11, hence act as zero by the same faithful comparison.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.1", "orig": "Weight vanishing", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Choose \\(\\mathcal T_h\\) acting trivially on \\(H^*(\\mathcal D)\\), put \\(d=m(t-1)\\), and \\( E_h^b=\\bigwedge^b(\\mathfrak m_{\\Lambda_h}/\\mathfrak m_{\\Lambda_h}^2)^*. \\) At \\(t=1\\), only \\(E_h^0=k\\) occurs.", "statement": "<p>Choose \\(\\mathcal T_h\\) acting trivially on \\(H^*(\\mathcal D)\\), put \\(d=m(t-1)\\), and\n\\[\nE_h^b=\\bigwedge^b(\\mathfrak m_{\\Lambda_h}/\\mathfrak m_{\\Lambda_h}^2)^*.\n\\]\nAt \\(t=1\\), only \\(E_h^0=k\\) occurs. Then\n\\[\nH^a(G,H_c^q(Z,\\mathscr A)\\otimes\\langle\\eta\\rangle\\otimes E_h^b)=0\n\\]\nunless \\(q=(t+2)m\\) and \\(b=d\\). At \\(b=d\\), extension to ambient support induces an equivalence, with remaining ambient coefficient a \\(G\\)-trivial line. Connected-frame actions are retained.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(\\epsilon_i\\) be diagonal Teichmüller weights. Translation Ext has row weights \\(e_i\\epsilon_i\\), \\(0\\leq e_i\\leq t-1\\), \\(\\sum e_i=b\\). A smallest \\(r\\)-plane contributes orientation \\(-t\\) in its first \\(r\\) rows, and volume contributes \\(+1\\) in every row. Thus coefficient exponents are\n\\[\n\\lambda_i=\n\\begin{cases}1-t+e_i,&amp;i\\leq r,\\\\1+e_i,&amp;i&gt;r.\\end{cases}\n\\]</p><p>For a compact flag stabilizer \\(P\\), restrict injectively to \\(P^+\\rtimes\\Delta\\), of prime-to-\\(p\\) index, where \\(\\Delta\\) is diagonal Teichmüller and \\(P^+\\) reduces upper unitriangular. Its ordered \\(p\\)-valuation gives upper entries value \\((j-i)/m\\), allowed lower principal entries \\(1+(j-i)/m\\), and principal diagonal entries \\(1\\). Since \\(1/m&gt;1/(p-1)\\), logarithm, exponential, and the required \\(p\\)-roots give the saturated valuation. The valued-group spectral sequence bounds cohomology weights by sums of distinct allowed cochain roots \\(\\epsilon_j-\\epsilon_i\\). Finite coefficient representations are filtered by their finite \\(p\\)-group augmentation ideal, so the same bound applies with added coefficient weights.</p><p>For \\(r&lt;m\\), the last \\(m-r\\) exponent sum is strictly positive; crossing allowed roots only increase it. Thus the integral weight is nonzero. Each coordinate has absolute value at most\n\\[\nt+m-1=n-1&lt;p-1,\n\\]\nso integral nonzero also excludes the trivial finite-torus character. Shapiro and the finite flag resolution kill all proper-plane terms.</p><p>For the ambient term \\(r=m\\), total exponent is \\(b-d\\), negative unless \\(b=d\\). At \\(b=d\\), every \\(e_i=t-1\\), and\n\\[\n(\\det)^{-t}(\\det)(\\det)^{t-1}=1\n\\]\nas actual \\(G\\)-characters. Proposition 5’s top ambient map is an isomorphism; its cone has only the killed proper-plane rows. Bounded hypercohomology proves the equivalence.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.2", "orig": "Equivariant top-edge insertion", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a natural \\(U_0\\)-equivariant, constant-cochain-linear map \\( \\mathcal D\\otimes_kE_h^d[-d]\\longrightarrow R\\Gamma(\\mathcal T_h,\\mathcal D), \\) which inserts a trivially acted-on cohomology row in translation …", "statement": "<p>There is a natural \\(U_0\\)-equivariant, constant-cochain-linear map\n\\[\n\\mathcal D\\otimes_kE_h^d[-d]\\longrightarrow R\\Gamma(\\mathcal T_h,\\mathcal D),\n\\]\nwhich inserts a trivially acted-on cohomology row in translation degree \\(d\\). After \\(R\\Gamma(G,-)\\), it is an equivalence retaining smooth \\(P_t\\)-action.</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(t&gt;1\\), finite Koszul duality gives\n\\[\nR\\operatorname{Hom}_\\Lambda(k,\\mathcal D)\n\\simeq R\\operatorname{Hom}_\\Lambda(k,\\Lambda)\\otimes_\\Lambda^L\\mathcal D\n\\simeq(k\\otimes_\\Lambda^L\\mathcal D)\\otimes_kE_h^d[-d].\n\\]\nThe augmentation \\(\\Lambda\\to k\\) gives the map. The top Ext identification is canonical for augmented automorphisms, including nonlinear frame changes. On an augmentation-annihilated row, the zero-differential Koszul model verifies top insertion.</p><p>Filter \\(\\mathcal D\\) by bounded cohomology. Lemma 12.1 kills all other translation columns after \\(G\\)-cohomology and almost extension, and identifies the top column on both sides. Bounded spectral sequences and faithful extension give the equivalence before extension. At \\(t=1\\), use the natural exact projection to trivial translation character.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.3", "orig": "Proposition 11.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Put \\(A_G=H_{\\mathrm{cts}}^*(G,k)\\) and \\(M_t^i=H^i(P_n,B_{\\mathrm{conn},G})\\).", "statement": "<p>Put \\(A_G=H_{\\mathrm{cts}}^*(G,k)\\) and \\(M_t^i=H^i(P_n,B_{\\mathrm{conn},G})\\). After tensoring with \\(R\\) and choosing bases of one-dimensional orientation factors,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hom}_k(M_t^i,k)\\otimes_kR\n\\simeq A_G^{m^2-i}\\otimes_kR.\n\\]\nThe transpose action of a constant \\(P_n\\)-class transported from \\(K_n\\) is cup product by its actual upper-left restriction to \\(G\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Set \\(C_G=R\\Gamma(G,R\\Gamma(\\mathcal T_h,\\mathcal D))\\). Proposition 8’s trace descent and connected corestriction give\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hom}_k(H^i(P_n,B_{\\mathrm{conn},G}^{(h)}),k)\n\\simeq H^{-i-t^2}(C_G).\n\\]\nThis finite Mittag–Leffler calculation precedes scalar extension. Power transport removes the root stage.</p><p>Use Lemma 12.2, the support comparison, Lemma 12.1, and ambient support. A \\(G\\)-cohomological degree \\(a\\) lies in dual degree\n\\[\na+(t+2)m+d+t^2-(m+n^2),\n\\]\nso primal degree is\n\\[\ni=n^2+m-(t+2)m-d-t^2-a=m^2-a.\n\\]\nAll remaining orientation and Tate factors are lines.</p><p>The support and top-insertion maps preserve the actual middle-frame constant action. Naturality of connected corestriction for shifted maps retains its underlying action after forgetting \\(P_t\\); no equivariant formality is used. Proposition 10 identifies this action on \\([Z/G]\\) with block restriction. The \\(G\\)-equivariant ambient support map preserves it, and the remaining line is \\(G\\)-trivial. Thus the final action is ordinary cup on \\(A_G\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "Exact tests of completed descent", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(p>n+1\\), \\(S=S_p^\\wedge\\), \\(D=L_{K(n)}S\\), and choose finite \\(k\\supseteq\\mathbb F_{p^a}\\), \\(1\\leq a\\leq n\\), with \\(f=[k:\\mathbb F_p]\\) prime to \\(p\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(p&gt;n+1\\), \\(S=S_p^\\wedge\\), \\(D=L_{K(n)}S\\), and choose finite \\(k\\supseteq\\mathbb F_{p^a}\\), \\(1\\leq a\\leq n\\), with \\(f=[k:\\mathbb F_p]\\) prime to \\(p\\). Put\n\\[\nG_n=P_n\\rtimes\\Gamma,\\qquad \\Gamma=\\operatorname{Gal}(k/\\mathbb F_p),\n\\qquad \\pi_0E_n=W(k)[[x_1,\\ldots,x_{n-1}]].\n\\]\nThe completed Amitsur terms are\n\\[\nA^q=L_{K(n)}(E_n^{\\wedge(q+1)})\n\\simeq\\operatorname{Map}^c(G_n^q,E_n),\n\\]\nwith semilinear first coface and the remaining group-nerve cofaces.</p><p>The augmentation \\(D\\to\\operatorname{Tot}A^\\bullet\\) is an equivalence. For every exact functor \\(F\\) from spectra to a stable infinity-category with countable limits,\n\\[\nF(D)\\xrightarrow{\\simeq}\\operatorname{Tot}F(A^\\bullet).\n\\]\nThe transformed totalization tower is quickly convergent. A spectrum-valued test with bounded \\(E_2\\) cohomological support has strong convergence and finite filtration in each total degree.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the structured completed-cooperation realization of Devinatz–Hopkins, including coherent cofaces, codegeneracies, multiplication, and augmentation. Transport their coordinates to standard inhomogeneous coordinates by\n\\[\n(T_qc)(g_1,\\ldots,g_q)\n=g_1^{-1}c(g_1g_2^{-1},\\ldots,g_{q-1}g_q^{-1},g_q).\n\\]\nThe inverse evaluates at successive suffix products and acts by the full product. Structured action and pullback intertwine all maps and preserve the unit.</p><p>For \\(k_0=\\mathbb F_{p^n}\\), extend to \\(k\\) by the finite unramified Galois equivalence\n\\[\nE_n(k)\\otimes_{E_n(k_0)}E_n(k)\\simeq\n\\prod_{\\sigma\\in\\operatorname{Gal}(k/k_0)}E_n(k).\n\\]\nFinite freeness removes Tor. Base change on both ends indexes factors by the lifts to \\(G_n\\); iteration gives the full diagram and retains the source \\(D\\).</p><p>Morava \\(E\\)-theory is descendable in the \\(K(n)\\)-local category. Its augmented partial-totalization tower has nilpotent error, or equivalently lies in the thick class generated by constant and nilpotent towers.</p><p>Each full partial totalization is a finite limit. The functor from nonempty subsets of \\(\\{0,\\ldots,s\\}\\) to their ordered sets in \\(\\Delta_{\\leq s}\\) is homotopy initial. Its comma complex at \\([q]\\) has simplices\n\\[\n(i_0,j_0),\\ldots,(i_b,j_b),\\quad\ni_0&lt;\\cdots&lt;i_b,\\quad j_0\\leq\\cdots\\leq j_b.\n\\]\nFor \\(s\\geq q\\), begin with the cone at \\((s,q)\\), then attach cones at \\((s,j)\\) in descending \\(j\\); their attaching complexes are contractible by induction. Thus the comma complex is contractible. The partial totalization is the punctured finite cube of unit insertions.</p><p>The inclusion of local spectra and then \\(F\\) preserve these finite limits and the fixed nilpotence of tower errors. Nilpotent towers have zero inverse limit, proving universal totalization. Quick convergence is preserved, and bounded cohomological support yields the stated finite strong-convergence filtration.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 14", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "[Common spherical representatives and ordinary residue comparison; original Proposition 12.2 and Lemmas 12.3–12.4.] Retain Proposition 13’s setting.", "statement": "<p>[Common spherical representatives and ordinary residue comparison; original Proposition 12.2 and Lemmas 12.3–12.4.]</p><p>Retain Proposition 13’s setting. There are common classes\n\\(y_i\\in\\pi_{1-2i}D\\), represented by continuous constant sphere cochains, whose integral Hurewicz restrictions to \\(P_n\\) are the transported \\(\\alpha_{n,i}^P\\). Their products represent the corresponding integral products and \\(y_\\varnothing\\) is the unit.</p><p>Fix \\(1\\leq t&lt;n\\), let \\(E_t=E_t(k)\\), and let\n\\[\n\\kappa_t=E_t/(p,u_1,\\ldots,u_{t-1})\n\\]\nbe the iterated cofiber residue module. Then\n\\[\n\\pi_*\\kappa_t=\\kappa_{t,*}=k[v^{\\pm1}],\\qquad |v|=2,\n\\]\nand \\(\\kappa_t\\) detects \\(K(t)\\)-equivalences. Ordinary residue smash of the completed descent terms has normalized coefficient complex\n\\[\nN^*\\pi_r(\\kappa_t\\wedge A^\\bullet)\n\\simeq\\kappa_{t,r}\\otimes_kC_{\\mathrm{cts}}^*(P_n;B_{n,t}),\n\\]\nzero for odd \\(r\\), preserving the unit and actual constant cup action. The coefficient ring is the algebraic connected-marking union with unmarked étale Tate module, not an analytic completion.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 14.1 gives the single family and its products. Lemmas 14.2–14.3 identify ordinary residue of the completed diagram, with exactly the algebraic topology, scalar action, and specified constant classes needed for all lower-height tests.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.1", "orig": "Proposition 12.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The common \\(y_i\\) and products asserted in Proposition 14 exist, with \\(y_\\varnothing=1\\).", "statement": "<p>The common \\(y_i\\) and products asserted in Proposition 14 exist, with \\(y_\\varnothing=1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Propositions 10–11 supply\n\\[\nu_i\\in\\pi_{1-2i}C_{\\mathrm{cts}}^*(P_n;S)\n\\]\nwith leading integral classes \\(\\alpha_{n,i}^P\\). The \\(\\Gamma\\)-action on integral constant cohomology is trivial by Proposition 10. Since \\(f\\) is a \\(p\\)-adic unit, normalized transfer lifts\n\\[\n\\overline u_i=\\frac1f\\sum_{\\gamma\\in\\Gamma}\\gamma u_i\n\\]\nto \\(G_n\\)-cochains without changing that Hurewicz image.</p><p>The unit \\(S\\to E_n\\) gives a multiplicative cosimplicial constant-section map\n\\[\nC_{\\mathrm{cts}}^\\bullet(G_n;S)\\to\n\\operatorname{Map}^c(G_n^\\bullet,E_n).\n\\]\nIt is formed at finite coefficient and Postnikov stages and extended by completeness; the coordinate transport of Proposition 13 commutes with it. Totalize and use Proposition 13 to obtain \\(y_i\\). Products give \\(y_I\\), and the empty product is the actual augmentation unit.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.2", "orig": "Lemma 12.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(R_n=W(k)[[x_1,\\ldots,x_{n-1}]]\\), with maximal ideal \\(\\mathfrak m\\), and let \\(Q\\) be profinite.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(R_n=W(k)[[x_1,\\ldots,x_{n-1}]]\\), with maximal ideal \\(\\mathfrak m\\), and let \\(Q\\) be profinite. Then\n\\[\nN_Q=C_{\\mathrm{cts}}(Q,R_n)\n\\]\nis complete and flat over \\(R_n\\), with\n\\[\nN_Q/\\mathfrak m^aN_Q=LC(Q,R_n/\\mathfrak m^a).\n\\]\nQuotienting by \\(p,x_1,\\ldots,x_{t-1}\\) gives\n\\(C_{\\mathrm{cts}}(Q,k[[x_t,\\ldots,x_{n-1}]])\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Functions into the finite quotient are constant on a finite clopen partition, so their values lift. Continuous coefficient division among finitely many monomial generators identifies the kernel; the same argument works for the height ideal. The reductions are filtered colimits of finite-free modules over \\(R_n/\\mathfrak m^a\\), hence flat. Their inverse limit is \\(N_Q\\). The Noetherian adic flatness criterion proves flatness; equivalently, successively lift a basis modulo \\(\\mathfrak m\\) to identify a completed free module.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.3", "orig": "Lemma 12.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For the completed terms of Proposition 13 and ordinary smash with \\(\\kappa_t\\), \\( N^*\\pi_r(\\kappa_t\\wedge A^\\bullet) \\simeq\\kappa_{t,r}\\otimes_kC_{\\mathrm{cts}}^*(P_n;B_{n,t}), \\) with common-stage bounded-pole …", "statement": "<p>For the completed terms of Proposition 13 and ordinary smash with \\(\\kappa_t\\),\n\\[\nN^*\\pi_r(\\kappa_t\\wedge A^\\bullet)\n\\simeq\\kappa_{t,r}\\otimes_kC_{\\mathrm{cts}}^*(P_n;B_{n,t}),\n\\]\nwith common-stage bounded-pole cochains, unit \\(1\\), and the action of Proposition 12.</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(M=\\operatorname{Map}^c(Q,E_n)\\),\n\\[\nE_t\\wedge M\\simeq(E_t\\wedge E_n)\\otimes_{E_n}M.\n\\]\nLemma 14.2 makes the Künneth spectral sequence Tor-free; the regular ring’s finite graded global dimension controls convergence:\n\\[\n\\pi_*(E_t\\wedge M)\n=\\pi_*(E_t\\wedge E_n)\\otimes_{E_{n,*}}C_{\\mathrm{cts}}(Q,E_{n,*}).\n\\]</p><p>Ordinary Landweber base change identifies the first factor with the two-sided formal-group-isomorphism cooperation algebra, including the periodic-frame linear coefficient. Flatness retains regularity of the test sequence. Under the universal isomorphism, successive height ideals agree and next generators differ by units, so residue imposes\n\\(p=x_1=\\cdots=x_{t-1}=0\\), and inverts \\(x_t\\). The resulting homotopy is even.</p><p>The test and source constants split as\n\\[\nk\\otimes_{\\mathbb F_p}k=\\prod_{\\sigma\\in\\Gamma}k.\n\\]\nLet \\(B_{n,t}^{[\\sigma]}\\) be the corresponding source-embedding marking ring. With test period \\(v\\) fixed,\n\\[\n\\pi_*(E_t\\wedge E_n)/(p,u_1,\\ldots,u_{t-1})\n\\simeq\\prod_{\\sigma\\in\\Gamma}\n(\\kappa_{t,*}\\otimes_kB_{n,t}^{[\\sigma]}).\n\\]\nIndeed, a Honda-to-source marking with linear coefficient \\(c\\) satisfies\n\\(x_tc^{p^t-1}=1\\); source period is \\(cv\\). Conversely this marking and period recover the strict cooperation isomorphism. No extra periodic frame or étale basis remains.</p><p>For a finite marking algebra \\(J=eB^a\\),\n\\[\nJ\\otimes_A C_{\\mathrm{cts}}(Q,A)\n=e(C_{\\mathrm{cts}}(Q,A)[1/x_t])^a.\n\\]\nThis is exactly finite-stage functions with common denominator and continuous finite-lattice coordinates. The algebraic union enforces one common marking stage; no post-localization completion occurs.</p><p>The first coface composes actual deformation-change isomorphisms and transports the marking by the stabilizer action. Finite coefficient formulas and compactness give common stages and pole bounds. Other cofaces and codegeneracies are nerve pullbacks. Units and products are the actual ones.</p><p>Finally \\(\\Gamma\\) permutes the source-embedding factors simply transitively. Their product is coinduced from \\(P_n\\), so Shapiro identifies the normalized \\(G_n\\)-complex with the \\(P_n\\)-complex of the identity factor. The test periodicity remains untwisted. This proves the claimed comparison and constant-action compatibility.</p><p>For scalar compatibility, each test parameter acts nullhomotopically on its cofiber because even regularity gives \\(\\pi_1(E_t/x)=0\\) and injectivity of endomorphism restriction to \\(E_t\\). Relative tensoring gives natural nullhomotopies on \\(\\kappa_t\\) and the entire tower. Thus tower homotopy is a \\(\\kappa_{t,*}\\)-module. The cofiber of \\(S_{(p)}\\to S_p^\\wedge\\) is rational and smashes trivially with \\(\\kappa_t\\); ordinary residue smash therefore agrees with the relative \\(S\\)-module test.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 15", "orig": "Simultaneous height bases", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The common classes from Proposition 14 satisfy, for every \\(1\\leq t<n\\), \\( L_{K(t)} \\left( \\bigvee_{I\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,n-t\\}} \\Sigma^{-d(I)}S\\xrightarrow{(y_I)}D \\right) \\) is an equivalence, with empty component the …", "statement": "<p>The common classes from Proposition 14 satisfy, for every \\(1\\leq t&lt;n\\),\n\\[\nL_{K(t)}\n\\left(\n\\bigvee_{I\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,n-t\\}}\n\\Sigma^{-d(I)}S\\xrightarrow{(y_I)}D\n\\right)\n\\]\nis an equivalence, with empty component the unit.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Proposition 13 to the exact functor \\(\\kappa_t\\wedge-\\). Proposition 14 gives the strongly convergent spectral sequence\n\\[\nE_2^{s,r}=\\kappa_{t,r}\\otimes_kH^s(P_n;B_{n,t})\n\\Longrightarrow\\pi_{r-s}(\\kappa_t\\wedge D).\n\\]\nProposition 12 makes it free over \\(\\kappa_{t,*}\\) on\n\\(\\xi_I\\cdot1\\), \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}\\), \\(m=n-t\\), in bidegrees \\((d(I),0)\\), and zero outside \\(0\\leq s\\leq m^2\\).</p><p>Locate each representative first in the connective source sphere-cochain tower, not the periodic residue tower. For \\(b=d(I)&gt;0\\), its \\((b-1)\\)-partial totalization is \\((1-b)\\)-connective and has no \\(\\pi_{-b}\\). The representative therefore lifts to the fiber, with leading filtration-\\(b\\) component its integral Hurewicz class \\(\\alpha_{n,I}^P\\). The multiplicative constant-section map and exact residue smash preserve partial totalizations and fibers. Proposition 14 identifies its leading residue component with precisely \\(\\xi_I\\cdot1\\). The unit supplies the empty case.</p><p>These representatives come from totalization and lift compatibly through every later stage, so their leading terms support no outgoing differentials. Scalar lifts from \\(\\pi_*E_t\\) give compatible representatives of every \\(\\kappa_{t,*}\\)-multiple. Thus every \\(E_2\\)-element lifts; induction on pages makes all differentials zero, including any possible incoming loss of the basis vectors.</p><p>Strong convergence gives a finite filtered \\(\\kappa_{t,*}\\)-module. The actual images of the \\(y_I\\) lift its associated-graded basis. Finite filtered linear algebra over the graded field makes them a basis of the abutment. Hence\n\\[\n\\bigvee_I\\Sigma^{-d(I)}\\kappa_t\\longrightarrow\\kappa_t\\wedge D\n\\]\nis an equivalence. Since \\(\\kappa_t\\) detects \\(K(t)\\)-equivalences, this proves the specified map.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 16", "orig": "Rational calculation of Barthel–Schlank–Stapleton–Weinstein, recorded as an external input", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every prime \\(p\\) and \\(n\\geq1\\), \\( \\pi_*L_{K(n)}S\\otimes_{\\mathbb Z}\\mathbb Q \\simeq\\bigwedge_{\\mathbb Q_p}(\\zeta_1,\\ldots,\\zeta_n), \\qquad |\\zeta_i|=1-2i, \\) with the scalar action induced by the unit.", "statement": "<p>For every prime \\(p\\) and \\(n\\geq1\\),\n\\[\n\\pi_*L_{K(n)}S\\otimes_{\\mathbb Z}\\mathbb Q\n\\simeq\\bigwedge_{\\mathbb Q_p}(\\zeta_1,\\ldots,\\zeta_n),\n\\qquad |\\zeta_i|=1-2i,\n\\]\nwith the scalar action induced by the unit. Consequently\n\\[\n\\dim_{\\mathbb Q_p}\\pi_{-b}L_0D\n=\\#\\{I\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}:d(I)=b\\}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>This is the cited rationalization theorem, [5, Theorem A], in homotopical grading. Replacing the sphere by its \\(p\\)-completion does not change positive-height localization. The exterior monomial basis gives the dimension formula. No identification of individual \\(\\zeta_i\\) with \\(y_i\\) is used.</p>"}], "edges": [[1, 0, "cite"], [2, 6, "cite"], [2, 7, "cite"], [2, 11, "cite"], [2, 14, "cite"], [2, 17, "cite"], [2, 37, "cite"], [3, 2, "cite"], [3, 5, "cite"], [4, 2, "cite"], [4, 5, "cite"], [5, 2, "cite"], [6, 11, "cite"], [6, 14, "cite"], [6, 27, "cite"], [6, 30, "cite"], [6, 32, "cite"], [6, 42, "cite"], [6, 47, "cite"], [7, 6, "cite"], [8, 6, "cite"], [9, 6, "cite"], [10, 6, "cite"], [11, 16, "cite"], [11, 26, "cite"], [11, 28, "cite"], [11, 30, "cite"], [11, 35, "cite"], [12, 11, "cite"], [12, 13, "cite"], [13, 11, "cite"], [14, 11, "cite"], [15, 11, "cite"], [16, 30, "cite"], [16, 32, "cite"], [16, 41, "cite"], [16, 43, "cite"], [16, 46, "cite"], [16, 49, "cite"], [16, 56, "cite"], [16, 69, "cite"], [17, 16, "cite"], [18, 16, "cite"], [18, 20, "cite"], [18, 28, "cite"], [19, 16, "cite"], [19, 20, "cite"], [20, 16, "cite"], [20, 21, "cite"], [20, 24, "cite"], [20, 28, "cite"], [21, 16, "cite"], [21, 22, "cite"], [22, 16, "cite"], [22, 24, "cite"], [23, 16, "cite"], [23, 24, "cite"], [24, 16, "cite"], [25, 16, "cite"], [25, 29, "cite"], [26, 16, "cite"], [26, 28, "cite"], [27, 16, "cite"], [27, 28, "cite"], [28, 16, "cite"], [28, 29, "cite"], [29, 16, "cite"], [30, 40, "cite"], [30, 51, "cite"], [31, 30, "cite"], [31, 32, "cite"], [32, 33, "cite"], [33, 30, "cite"], [34, 30, "cite"], [35, 40, "cite"], [35, 51, "cite"], [36, 35, "cite"], [36, 37, "cite"], [37, 35, "cite"], [37, 38, "cite"], [38, 35, "cite"], [39, 35, "cite"], [39, 50, "cite"], [40, 51, "cite"], [40, 71, "cite"], [41, 40, "cite"], [41, 43, "cite"], [42, 40, "cite"], [42, 46, "cite"], [42, 47, "cite"], [43, 40, "cite"], [43, 46, "cite"], [44, 40, "cite"], [44, 46, "cite"], [45, 40, "cite"], [45, 46, "cite"], [45, 48, "cite"], [46, 40, "cite"], [46, 48, "cite"], [46, 50, "cite"], [47, 40, "cite"], [47, 48, "cite"], [48, 40, "cite"], [48, 49, "cite"], [49, 40, "cite"], [49, 50, "cite"], [50, 40, "cite"], [51, 68, "cite"], [52, 58, "cite"], [52, 62, "cite"], [52, 68, "cite"], [52, 71, "cite"], [52, 74, "cite"], [53, 52, "cite"], [53, 54, "cite"], [54, 52, "cite"], [55, 52, "cite"], [56, 52, "cite"], [56, 57, "cite"], [57, 52, "cite"], [58, 68, "cite"], [58, 74, "cite"], [59, 61, "cite"], [60, 63, "cite"], [61, 62, "cite"], [61, 66, "cite"], [62, 58, "cite"], [63, 66, "cite"], [64, 66, "cite"], [65, 66, "cite"], [66, 58, "cite"], [67, 58, "cite"], [68, 76, "cite"], [68, 77, "cite"], [69, 70, "cite"], [69, 71, "cite"], [70, 71, "cite"], [71, 68, "cite"], [72, 73, "cite"], [72, 74, "cite"], [72, 76, "cite"], [72, 77, "cite"], [73, 0, "cite"], [73, 77, "cite"], [74, 73, "cite"], [75, 73, "cite"], [75, 76, "cite"], [76, 73, "cite"], [77, 0, "cite"], [78, 0, "cite"]]});
